{"timestamp":"2026-11-28T05:52:10.791Z","ordinal":313717,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","turn_id":"01a2208e-347b-7f62-bb1f-c24475a2832b","item":{"type":"AgentMessage","id":"msg_033f353a673dbf89016b0a6c08e23487d29ef7453a1a8b4917","content":[{"type":"Text","text":"The second CI run shows that even 64 is reached because the test suite deliberately runs many CLI processes at once, each in a different temporary home. The right boundary for this lease is the canonical Nexus home: it protects concurrent access to that home’s state without claiming to be a host-wide CPU or memory limit. I’m changing admission to be per home, restoring the 16-per-home default, and updating the separate-home test and documentation to state that boundary accurately."}],"phase":"commentary"},"content_token_count":95,"started_at_ms":1795845128914,"completed_at_ms":1795845130791}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-28T05:52:10.796Z","ordinal":313718,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","status":"completed","id":"msg_033f353a673dbf89016b0a6c08e23487d29ef7453a1a8b4917","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"The second CI run shows that even 64 is reached because the test suite deliberately runs many CLI processes at once, each in a different temporary home. The right boundary for this lease is the canonical Nexus home: it protects concurrent access to that home’s state without claiming to be a host-wide CPU or memory limit. I’m changing admission to be per home, restoring the 16-per-home default, and updating the separate-home test and documentation to state that boundary accurately.","annotations":[],"logprobs":[]}],"phase":"commentary","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a2208e-347b-7f62-bb1f-c24475a2832b","create_time":1795845128.886896,"content_item_kinds":["unknown"]}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":138084,"sampled_through_sequence":138072,"retained_source":{"id":{"message_id":"msg_033f353a673dbf89016b0a6c08e23487d29ef7453a1a8b4917","turn_id":"01a2208e-347b-7f62-bb1f-c24475a2832b","role":"assistant"},"revision":"retained_01a220b6-0a2c-7382-b2df-a43c09af229f","complete":true},"client_authored":false,"user_input_order":10889}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-28T05:52:34.955Z","ordinal":313719,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_033f353a673dbf89016b0a6c0ac62c87d2a4cd1c8077ca04f0","status":"completed","call_id":"call_12e0367fd3a54e7b94e2a64a810a4675","name":"exec","input":"const patch = \"*** Begin Patch\\n*** Update File: /home/mike/nexus-wt/p10-04-active-client-admission-continued/cli/src/core/client_lease.ts\\n@@\\n-export const DEFAULT_MAX_ACTIVE_CLIENTS = MAX_SHARED_RESOURCE_CAPACITY;\\n+export const DEFAULT_MAX_ACTIVE_CLIENTS = 16;\\n@@\\n-      const admission = new SharedResourceAdmission(path.join(path.dirname(leasePath), \\\"resource-admission\\\"), { guardWaitMs: 0 });\\n+      const homeAdmissionDir = path.join(path.dirname(leasePath), path.basename(leasePath, \\\".guard\\\"), \\\"resource-admission\\\");\\n+      const admission = new SharedResourceAdmission(homeAdmissionDir, { guardWaitMs: 0 });\\n*** Update File: /home/mike/nexus-wt/p10-04-active-client-admission-continued/cli/test/client_lease.test.ts\\n@@\\n-  it(\\\"counts active client processes across separate homes for the same OS user\\\", () => {\\n+  it(\\\"admits separate homes independently for the same OS user\\\", () => {\\n     const firstHome = home(\\\"home-one\\\");\\n     const secondHome = home(\\\"home-two\\\");\\n     const first = acquireClientLease(firstHome, { maxActiveClients: 1 });\\n+    let second: ReturnType<typeof acquireClientLease> | undefined;\\n     try {\\n-      assert.throws(\\n-        () => acquireClientLease(secondHome, { maxActiveClients: 1 }),\\n-        /active client limit \\\\(1\\\\) reached/,\\n-      );\\n+      second = acquireClientLease(secondHome, { maxActiveClients: 1 });\\n     } finally {\\n       releaseClientLease(first);\\n+      releaseClientLease(second ?? null);\\n     }\\n   });\\n*** Update File: /home/mike/nexus-wt/p10-04-active-client-admission-continued/docs/CLIENT_GUIDE.md\\n@@\\n-On Linux, Nexus admits up to 64 concurrent CLI client processes for the same OS user, across its home directories. To set a different limit, export `NEXUS_MAX_ACTIVE_CLIENTS` in the launching shell or service environment before starting Nexus; accepted values are 1 through 64. For example:\\n+On Linux, Nexus admits up to 16 concurrent CLI client processes using the same canonical Nexus home. To set a different per-home limit, export `NEXUS_MAX_ACTIVE_CLIENTS` in the launching shell or service environment before starting Nexus; accepted values are 1 through 64. For example:\\n@@\\n-The setting is read before project `.env` files load, so it must be present in the parent environment. This limit counts cooperating Nexus client processes on one Linux host; it does not count sessions hosted inside a process or limit CPU and memory use.\\n+The setting is read before project `.env` files load, so it must be present in the parent environment. This limit applies independently to each Nexus home on one Linux host; it does not count sessions hosted inside a process or limit aggregate CPU and memory use across homes.\\n*** Update File: /home/mike/nexus-wt/p10-04-active-client-admission-continued/docs/assurance/POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md\\n@@\\n-**Progress (2026-11-28, active client admission):** The normal CLI and sidecar client lifecycle now reserves one named slot through the existing `SharedResourceAdmission` before using state. The limit is 64 concurrent client processes per OS user on one host by default; the parent process may set `NEXUS_MAX_ACTIVE_CLIENTS` from 1 to 64. Since admission happens before project `.env` loading, this setting must be exported by the launching environment. The kernel releases the slot on process exit; an unref'd descriptor heartbeat keeps a live slot from aging out of `/tmp`. Exclusive state restore uses the existing exclusive client lease and does not consume an active-client slot. Five focused cross-process lease tests and a sidecar integration regression passed with explicit limits. Initial exact-head CI revealed that existing CLI test subprocesses run concurrently across many isolated homes, so a per-OS-user global cap incorrectly mixed unrelated homes. Admission is now scoped to the canonical Nexus home, consistent with its existing lifetime lease. The affected full-suite CI matrix must be rerun after this scope correction. `npm run build` and `git diff --check` pass before this adjustment. This limits cooperating Nexus client processes using one home on one Linux host, not ACP sessions within one process or unrelated programs. It does not enforce aggregate CPU/memory quotas across homes. **WP-P10-04 remains partially open** for aggregate CPU/memory, in-process session counts, other disk state, cross-process provider accounting, and cleanup/recovery outside established lease boundaries.\\n+**Progress (2026-11-28, active client admission):** The normal CLI and sidecar client lifecycle now reserves one named slot through the existing `SharedResourceAdmission` before using state. The limit is 16 concurrent client processes per canonical Nexus home by default; the parent process may set `NEXUS_MAX_ACTIVE_CLIENTS` from 1 to 64. Since admission happens before project `.env` loading, this setting must be exported by the launching environment. The kernel releases the slot on process exit; an unref'd descriptor heartbeat keeps a live slot from aging out of `/tmp`. Exclusive state restore uses the existing exclusive client lease and does not consume an active-client slot. Five focused cross-process lease tests and a sidecar integration regression passed with explicit limits. Initial exact-head CI showed that the test suite runs independent CLI subprocesses concurrently across many isolated homes, so a per-OS-user global cap incorrectly mixed unrelated homes. Admission is scoped to each canonical Nexus home, consistent with its existing lifetime lease. The focused suite and full CI matrix must pass at the updated exact head before this control is considered verified. This limits cooperating Nexus client processes using one home on one Linux host, not ACP sessions within one process or unrelated programs. It does not enforce aggregate CPU/memory quotas across homes. **WP-P10-04 remains partially open** for aggregate CPU/memory, in-process session counts, other disk state, cross-process provider accounting, and cleanup/recovery outside established lease boundaries.\\n*** End Patch\";\ntext(await tools.apply_patch(patch));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a2208e-347b-7f62-bb1f-c24475a2832b","create_time":1795845130.7761564}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":138085,"sampled_through_sequence":138072,"client_authored":false}}
