{"timestamp":"2026-11-28T05:52:40.243Z","ordinal":313728,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a220b6-7d33-7853-9db6-90c262690737","call_id":"call_8e56e180e73149129dd03695dd1e8679","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.7 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"docs/assurance/POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md:157:**Progress (2026-11-28, active client admission):** The normal CLI and sidecar client lifecycle now reserves one named slot through the existing `SharedResourceAdmission` before using state. The limit is 64 concurrent client processes per OS user on one host by default; the parent process may set `NEXUS_MAX_ACTIVE_CLIENTS` from 1 to 64. Since admission happens before project `.env` loading, this setting must be exported by the launching environment. The kernel releases the slot on process exit; an unref'd descriptor heartbeat keeps a live slot from aging out of `/tmp`. Exclusive state restore uses the existing exclusive client lease and does not consume an active-client slot. Five focused cross-process lease tests and a sidecar integration regression passed with explicit limits. Initial exact-head CI revealed that the previous default of 16 blocked existing CLI test subprocesses that run concurrently, so the default now uses the existing shared-resource hard maximum of 64; the affected full-suite CI matrix must be rerun. `npm run build` and `git diff --check` pass before this adjustment. This limits cooperating Nexus client processes on one Linux host, not ACP sessions within one process or unrelated programs. It does not enforce CPU/memory quotas. **WP-P10-04 remains partially open** for aggregate CPU/memory, in-process session counts, other disk state, cross-process provider accounting, and cleanup/recovery outside established lease boundaries.\ndocs/CLIENT_GUIDE.md:34:On Linux, Nexus admits up to 64 concurrent CLI client processes for the same OS user, across its home directories. To set a different limit, export `NEXUS_MAX_ACTIVE_CLIENTS` in the launching shell or service environment before starting Nexus; accepted values are 1 through 64. For example:\ncli/test/client_lease.test.ts:67:  it(\"counts active client processes across separate homes for the same OS user\", () => {\ncli/src/core/client_lease.ts:11:export const DEFAULT_MAX_ACTIVE_CLIENTS = MAX_SHARED_RESOURCE_CAPACITY;\ncli/src/core/client_lease.ts:22:  const value = options.maxActiveClients ?? (raw === undefined ? DEFAULT_MAX_ACTIVE_CLIENTS : Number(raw));\ncli/src/core/client_lease.ts:108:      const admission = new SharedResourceAdmission(path.join(path.dirname(leasePath), \"resource-admission\"), { guardWaitMs: 0 });\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a2208e-347b-7f62-bb1f-c24475a2832b","create_time":1795845160.2431679}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":138089,"sampled_through_sequence":138086,"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
