{"timestamp":"2026-11-28T05:53:07.711Z","ordinal":313739,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_033f353a673dbf89016b0a6c37c41887d2b53ea69deb1acb39","status":"completed","call_id":"call_62e553a661b9451fb0d3765e1bef75d9","name":"exec","input":"const patch = \"*** Begin Patch\\n*** Update File: /home/mike/nexus-wt/p10-04-active-client-admission-continued/docs/assurance/POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md\\n@@\\n-**Progress (2026-11-28, active client admission):** The normal CLI and sidecar client lifecycle now reserves one named slot through the existing `SharedResourceAdmission` before using state. The limit is 64 concurrent client processes per OS user on one host by default; the parent process may set `NEXUS_MAX_ACTIVE_CLIENTS` from 1 to 64. Since admission happens before project `.env` loading, this setting must be exported by the launching environment. The kernel releases the slot on process exit; an unref'd descriptor heartbeat keeps a live slot from aging out of `/tmp`. Exclusive state restore uses the existing exclusive client lease and does not consume an active-client slot. Five focused cross-process lease tests and a sidecar integration regression passed with explicit limits. Initial exact-head CI revealed that the previous default of 16 blocked existing CLI test subprocesses that run concurrently, so the default now uses the existing shared-resource hard maximum of 64; the affected full-suite CI matrix must be rerun. `npm run build` and `git diff --check` pass before this adjustment. This limits cooperating Nexus client processes on one Linux host, not ACP sessions within one process or unrelated programs. It does not enforce CPU/memory quotas. **WP-P10-04 remains partially open** for aggregate CPU/memory, in-process session counts, other disk state, cross-process provider accounting, and cleanup/recovery outside established lease boundaries.\\n+**Progress (2026-11-28, active client admission):** The normal CLI and sidecar client lifecycle now reserves one named slot through the existing `SharedResourceAdmission` before using state. The limit is 16 concurrent client processes using the same canonical Nexus home by default; the parent process may set `NEXUS_MAX_ACTIVE_CLIENTS` from 1 to 64. Since admission happens before project `.env` loading, this setting must be exported by the launching environment. The kernel releases the slot on process exit; an unref'd descriptor heartbeat keeps a live slot from aging out of `/tmp`. Exclusive state restore uses the existing exclusive client lease and does not consume an active-client slot. Five focused cross-process lease tests and a sidecar integration regression passed with explicit limits. Initial exact-head CI showed that the test suite runs independent CLI subprocesses concurrently across many isolated homes, so a per-OS-user global cap incorrectly mixed unrelated homes. Admission is scoped to each canonical Nexus home, consistent with its existing lifetime lease. The focused suite and full CI matrix must pass at the updated exact head before this control is considered verified. This limits cooperating Nexus client processes using one home on one Linux host, not ACP sessions within one process or unrelated programs. It does not enforce aggregate CPU/memory quotas across homes. **WP-P10-04 remains partially open** for aggregate CPU/memory, in-process session counts, other disk state, cross-process provider accounting, and cleanup/recovery outside established lease boundaries.\\n*** End Patch\";\ntext(await tools.apply_patch(patch));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a2208e-347b-7f62-bb1f-c24475a2832b","create_time":1795845175.7697515}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":138094,"sampled_through_sequence":138092,"client_authored":false}}
