{"timestamp":"2026-11-28T06:00:35.268Z","ordinal":313893,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","turn_id":"01a2208e-347b-7f62-bb1f-c24475a2832b","item":{"type":"AgentMessage","id":"msg_033f353a673dbf89016b0a6e01c86887d2b3c989f3b21f7e6c","content":[{"type":"Text","text":"The latest CI confirms the home boundary is correct, but the default of 16 is still too low for existing tests that intentionally run more than 16 clients against one home concurrently. I’m keeping the per-home boundary and setting its default to the supported hard ceiling of 64. This retains the finite cap and avoids treating unrelated homes as one pool."}],"phase":"commentary"},"content_token_count":71,"started_at_ms":1795845633811,"completed_at_ms":1795845635268}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-28T06:00:35.273Z","ordinal":313894,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","status":"completed","id":"msg_033f353a673dbf89016b0a6e01c86887d2b3c989f3b21f7e6c","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"The latest CI confirms the home boundary is correct, but the default of 16 is still too low for existing tests that intentionally run more than 16 clients against one home concurrently. I’m keeping the per-home boundary and setting its default to the supported hard ceiling of 64. This retains the finite cap and avoids treating unrelated homes as one pool.","annotations":[],"logprobs":[]}],"phase":"commentary","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a2208e-347b-7f62-bb1f-c24475a2832b","create_time":1795845633.7865887,"content_item_kinds":["unknown"]}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":138166,"sampled_through_sequence":138164,"retained_source":{"id":{"message_id":"msg_033f353a673dbf89016b0a6e01c86887d2b3c989f3b21f7e6c","turn_id":"01a2208e-347b-7f62-bb1f-c24475a2832b","role":"assistant"},"revision":"retained_01a220bd-bcc9-7d72-8751-1ca77f9e56dd","complete":true},"client_authored":false,"user_input_order":10891}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-28T06:00:51.467Z","ordinal":313895,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_033f353a673dbf89016b0a6e033dc487d2a8f6ed0fc506e946","status":"completed","call_id":"call_f74ac7e626954635af193663351987b3","name":"exec","input":"const patch = \"*** Begin Patch\\n*** Update File: /home/mike/nexus-wt/p10-04-active-client-admission-continued/cli/src/core/client_lease.ts\\n@@\\n-export const DEFAULT_MAX_ACTIVE_CLIENTS = 16;\\n+export const DEFAULT_MAX_ACTIVE_CLIENTS = MAX_SHARED_RESOURCE_CAPACITY;\\n*** Update File: /home/mike/nexus-wt/p10-04-active-client-admission-continued/docs/CLIENT_GUIDE.md\\n@@\\n-On Linux, Nexus admits up to 16 concurrent CLI client processes using the same canonical Nexus home. To set a different per-home limit, export `NEXUS_MAX_ACTIVE_CLIENTS` in the launching shell or service environment before starting Nexus; accepted values are 1 through 64. For example:\\n+On Linux, Nexus admits up to 64 concurrent CLI client processes using the same canonical Nexus home. To set a different per-home limit, export `NEXUS_MAX_ACTIVE_CLIENTS` in the launching shell or service environment before starting Nexus; accepted values are 1 through 64. For example:\\n*** Update File: /home/mike/nexus-wt/p10-04-active-client-admission-continued/docs/assurance/POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md\\n@@\\n-**Progress (2026-11-28, active client admission):** The normal CLI and sidecar client lifecycle now reserves one named slot through the existing `SharedResourceAdmission` before using state. The limit is 16 concurrent client processes using the same canonical Nexus home by default; the parent process may set `NEXUS_MAX_ACTIVE_CLIENTS` from 1 to 64. Since admission happens before project `.env` loading, this setting must be exported by the launching environment. The kernel releases the slot on process exit; an unref'd descriptor heartbeat keeps a live slot from aging out of `/tmp`. Exclusive state restore uses the existing exclusive client lease and does not consume an active-client slot. Five focused cross-process lease tests and a sidecar integration regression passed with explicit limits. Initial exact-head CI showed that the test suite runs independent CLI subprocesses concurrently across many isolated homes, so a per-OS-user global cap incorrectly mixed unrelated homes. Admission is scoped to each canonical Nexus home, consistent with its existing lifetime lease. The focused suite and full CI matrix must pass at the updated exact head before this control is considered verified. This limits cooperating Nexus client processes using one home on one Linux host, not ACP sessions within one process or unrelated programs. It does not enforce aggregate CPU/memory quotas across homes. **WP-P10-04 remains partially open** for aggregate CPU/memory, in-process session counts, other disk state, cross-process provider accounting, and cleanup/recovery outside established lease boundaries.\\n+**Progress (2026-11-28, active client admission):** The normal CLI and sidecar client lifecycle now reserves one named slot through the existing `SharedResourceAdmission` before using state. The limit is 64 concurrent client processes using the same canonical Nexus home by default; the parent process may lower `NEXUS_MAX_ACTIVE_CLIENTS` from 1 to 64. Since admission happens before project `.env` loading, this setting must be exported by the launching environment. The kernel releases the slot on process exit; an unref'd descriptor heartbeat keeps a live slot from aging out of `/tmp`. Exclusive state restore uses the existing exclusive client lease and does not consume an active-client slot. Five focused cross-process lease tests and a sidecar integration regression passed with explicit limits. Initial exact-head CI showed that a per-OS-user global cap incorrectly mixed unrelated homes, while a default of 16 also constrained existing same-home integration tests that run many CLI subprocesses concurrently. Admission is scoped to each canonical Nexus home and defaults to the existing shared-resource hard maximum of 64. The full CI matrix must pass at the updated exact head before this control is considered verified. This limits cooperating Nexus client processes using one home on one Linux host, not ACP sessions within one process or unrelated programs. It does not enforce aggregate CPU/memory quotas across homes. **WP-P10-04 remains partially open** for aggregate CPU/memory, in-process session counts, other disk state, cross-process provider accounting, and cleanup/recovery outside established lease boundaries.\\n*** End Patch\";\ntext(await tools.apply_patch(patch));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a2208e-347b-7f62-bb1f-c24475a2832b","create_time":1795845635.2458851}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":138167,"sampled_through_sequence":138164,"client_authored":false}}
