{"timestamp":"2026-11-28T07:30:57.993Z","ordinal":316257,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_033f353a673dbf89016b0a831d2cb487d2b482fd502b31a364","status":"completed","call_id":"call_1470b667e8a34c9fa51de94b559c6381","name":"exec","input":"const patch = \"*** Begin Patch\\n*** Update File: /home/mike/nexus-wt/p10-04-client-admission-evidence/docs/assurance/POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md\\n@@\\n-**Progress (2026-11-28, active client admission):** The normal CLI and sidecar client lifecycle now reserves one named slot through the existing `SharedResourceAdmission` before using state. The limit is 64 concurrent client processes using the same canonical Nexus home by default; the parent process may lower `NEXUS_MAX_ACTIVE_CLIENTS` from 1 to 64. Since admission happens before project `.env` loading, this setting must be exported by the launching environment. The kernel releases the slot on process exit; an unref'd descriptor heartbeat keeps a live slot from aging out of `/tmp`. Exclusive state restore uses the existing exclusive client lease and does not consume an active-client slot. Five focused cross-process lease tests and a sidecar integration regression passed with explicit limits. Initial exact-head CI showed that a per-OS-user global cap incorrectly mixed unrelated homes, while a default of 16 also constrained existing same-home integration tests that run many CLI subprocesses concurrently. Admission is scoped to each canonical Nexus home and defaults to the existing shared-resource hard maximum of 64. The full CI matrix must pass at the updated exact head before this control is considered verified. This limits cooperating Nexus client processes using one home on one Linux host, not ACP sessions within one process or unrelated programs. It does not enforce aggregate CPU/memory quotas across homes. **WP-P10-04 remains partially open** for aggregate CPU/memory, in-process session counts, other disk state, cross-process provider accounting, and cleanup/recovery outside established lease boundaries.\\n-\\n-**Progress (2026-11-28, merged PR #934):** Linux CLI clients sharing one canonical home now use the existing kernel-backed client lease and `SharedResourceAdmission` to enforce a configurable process cap, defaulting to 64. The admission guard waits at most 10 seconds so simultaneous starts do not turn lock contention into a false “client limit reached” result; genuine slot exhaustion still refuses admission. A 16-process same-home regression reproduced the false refusal before the wait-bound fix and passed in three consecutive runs afterward on Node 24.11.1. `npm run build` passed. Required exact-head checks passed at PR head `4f55c6d4bfd4a58f480816b5871d5b97c36a0221`: both Node test matrices and all eight shards, Linux build, JavaScript/TypeScript, and CodeQL. PR #934 merged at `2026-11-28T07:16:02Z` as `9b415dfdf8b754dd67a6cbb7b0a5084a1bfe9395`; post-merge CI run `46120407959` and CodeQL run `46120408066` passed on that exact merge commit. Entire Gates failed on the PR head, but is advisory and not a required merge check. This bounds cooperating Nexus CLI processes per canonical home on one Linux host; it does not bound sessions inside a process, resource consumption, separate homes, or non-Nexus processes. **WP-P10-04 remains partially open** for the other aggregate resource, provider-accounting, and cleanup/recovery gaps above.\\n+**Progress (2026-11-28, merged PR #934):** The normal CLI and sidecar lifecycle reserve a named slot through the existing `SharedResourceAdmission` before using state. Admission is scoped per canonical home and defaults to 64 concurrent client processes; the launching environment may lower `NEXUS_MAX_ACTIVE_CLIENTS` from 1 to 64 because admission runs before project `.env` loading. The kernel releases slots on process exit, and the existing exclusive restore lease remains unchanged. A 16-process same-home regression reproduced false capacity errors under zero-wait guard contention, then passed in three consecutive runs with the bounded 10-second guard wait; genuine slot exhaustion still fails closed. `npm run build` passed on Node 24.11.1. Required exact-head checks passed at PR head `4f55c6d4bfd4a58f480816b5871d5b97c36a0221`: both Node test matrices, all eight test shards, Linux build, JavaScript/TypeScript, and CodeQL. PR #934 merged at `2026-11-28T07:16:02Z` as `9b415dfdf8b754dd67a6cbb7b0a5084a1bfe9395`; post-merge CI run `46120407959` and CodeQL run `46120408066` passed on that exact merge commit. Entire Gates failed on the PR head, but is advisory and not a required merge check. This bounds cooperating Nexus CLI processes per canonical home on one Linux host, not in-process sessions, aggregate CPU/memory, separate homes, or non-Nexus processes. **WP-P10-04 remains partially open** for the other aggregate resource, provider-accounting, and cleanup/recovery gaps above.\\n*** End Patch\";\ntext(await tools.apply_patch(patch));\nconst r=await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"git diff --check && git diff --stat && git status --short\",workdir:\"/home/mike/nexus-wt/p10-04-client-admission-evidence\",yield_time_ms:10000,max_output_tokens:500});text(r.output)\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a220e5-c285-7e72-9bb5-c6a32a48db70","create_time":1795851037.1779153}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":139249,"sampled_through_sequence":139247,"client_authored":false}}
