{"timestamp":"2026-11-28T08:28:15.665Z","ordinal":317824,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_033f353a673dbf89016b0a909dd26887d2b229ea870e0de439","status":"completed","call_id":"call_d0f45ae9b89f453ebfac44ebf85ddf4e","name":"exec","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"rg -n -A45 -B5 'WP-P10-02|WP-P10-04' docs/assurance/POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md; git status --short --branch; git rev-parse HEAD\",\"workdir\":\"/home/mike/nexus-wt/p10-02-file-side-effect-crash-recovery\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":4000}); text(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a22143-fcf9-7462-927e-e66d27ee2285","create_time":1795854493.8251476}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":139976,"sampled_through_sequence":139974,"client_authored":false}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-28T08:28:15.917Z","ordinal":317825,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","turn_id":"01a22143-fcf9-7462-927e-e66d27ee2285","session_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","root_turn_id":"01a22143-fcf9-7462-927e-e66d27ee2285","response_id":"resp_033f353a673dbf89016b0a9095b14887d2a69b9061f98db127","usage":{"input_tokens":163269,"cached_input_tokens":162816,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":516,"reasoning_output_tokens":404,"total_tokens":163785},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":651417,"cached_input_tokens":647808,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":1002,"reasoning_output_tokens":491,"total_tokens":652419},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":6606722581,"cached_input_tokens":6532473600,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":15246849,"reasoning_output_tokens":8979044,"total_tokens":6621969430}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-28T08:28:18.607Z","ordinal":317826,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","turn_id":"01a22143-fcf9-7462-927e-e66d27ee2285","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-5c871806-e1a8-4762-908e-bc3a24d7dd00","process_id":"48283","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","rg -n -A45 -B5 'WP-P10-02|WP-P10-04' docs/assurance/POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md; git status --short --branch; git rev-parse HEAD"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-wt/p10-02-file-side-effect-crash-recovery","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"rg -n -A45 -B5 'WP-P10-02|WP-P10-04' docs/assurance/POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md; git status --short --branch; git rev-parse HEAD"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"22-\n23-**Required tests / acceptance:** Record the configured ruleset and required status checks at the exact revision; report independent approval as unavailable. This package is not a prerequisite for implementation, PR publication, or release work under the current owner decision.\n24-\n25-**Migration / rollback:** No repository policy change is proposed. Residual risk: a sole maintainer can change acceptance code and its checks; exact-head hosted CI and candid self-review do not provide independent review.\n26-\n27:## WP-P10-02 — Durable task-level execution budgets\n28-\n29-**Priority:** P0 · **Complexity:** L · **Dependencies:** WP-P10-01 recommended first; can be implemented independently.\n30-\n31-**Gap/evidence:** Direct objective continuation has finite total-step, cumulative objective elapsed-time, and per-invocation time ceilings (`cli/src/types.ts`, `cli/src/core/run_turn.ts`, `cli/src/core/execution_tracking.ts`). Durable operations enforce aggregate active wall time across their driver interval and, when an optional token cap is configured, roll up child token usage and fail closed when measured usage is unavailable. PR #857 also puts provider retries, same-turn side requests, and in-process nested delegates under the persisted inference-submission counter for a durable objective. PR #869 adds a separate durable-operation counter shared across its work units. A single persisted accounting scope across every provider call path has not been demonstrated; calls made outside those objective or operation scopes and arbitrary side-effect outcomes after process or host failure still require investigation and reconciliation.\n32-\n33-**Affected surfaces:** `types.ts`, `config.ts`, `run_turn.ts`, subagent execution in `agent.ts`, provider recovery, tool deadlines, durable objective/task state, operation runner and CLI/UI reporting.\n34-\n35-**Design / reuse:** Extend `ContinuationBudget` and existing durable objective/operation records with a task budget id, maximum total steps/tool invocations/inference submissions/elapsed time/tokens, per-tool deadline and delegation depth. Persist consumed counters before/after side effects with `withDurableSync`. Child agents inherit the parent’s remaining budget; provider retry consumes the same inference allowance. A resumed task cannot regain budget. Require explicit operator-authorized budget renewal after exhaustion.\n36-\n37-**Implementation steps:**\n38-\n39-1. Define additive schema fields with migration defaults that do not silently claim prior counters are zero; old tasks resume in a conservative bounded mode or require explicit operator choice.\n40-2. Add a shared budget tracker passed through direct turns, transient in-turn subagents, durable work units, provider recovery and tool dispatch.\n41-3. Enforce before direct and delegated dispatch and before any retry; classify exhaustion as `BLOCKED`/`LIMIT_REACHED`, never `VERIFIED` or successful operation completion.\n42-4. Add ceilings for response streaming and whole-turn wall time, including a finite total timeout for local inference streams; idle timeout remains separate.\n43-5. Report dimension, configured limit, consumed amount and resume behavior in CLI/TUI result.\n44-\n45-**Required tests / acceptance:** Model emits distinct writes indefinitely: run stops at exact step/tool/token/time budget with nonzero/non-success result. Nested subagent shares remaining budget. Repeated provider failures consume retry allowance. Exhaustion followed by process restart and resume does not reset counters. Resume near limit permits only remaining budget. A hung local tool and slow-drip stream stop by configured deadline. Budget persistence write failure prevents new side-effectful dispatch.\n46-\n47-**Migration / rollback:** New config defaults may shorten formerly unlimited runs; provide an explicit operator override with a hard documented maximum, not Infinity. Keep old records readable. Rollback must not turn persisted exhausted state into zero; older binaries should refuse unsupported budget schema versions. Residual risk: already-running external calls may finish after cancellation or have unknown outcomes.\n48-\n49-**Progress (2026-11-22):** The `runTurnImpl` path persists a per-objective tool invocation ceiling (default 1,000; hard maximum 10,000), admits calls before dispatch, and checkpoints the count before dispatcher effects. In-turn `runSubagent` children reserve from the same tracker and persist each reservation before dispatch; exhaustion and failed persistence both stop the child before another effect. A saved ceiling cannot be raised by a later invocation. Legacy execution records explicitly mark tool history incomplete, so migrated counts are not presented as exact. Tests cover bounded direct and delegated calls, resume without budget renewal, admission bounds, legacy lower-bound tracking, failed delegated persistence, and workspace-observer classification. This tranche does not yet cover durable work units, provider recovery/retries, inference or token accounting, cumulative elapsed limits, per-tool deadlines, or process-crash reconciliation.\n50-\n51-**Progress (2026-11-23, merged PR #760):** The primary turn's provider-recovery loop reserves each request, including retries, against a persisted objective ceiling (default 1,000; hard maximum 10,000). Persistence failure prevents dispatch; resume can lower but not raise the saved ceiling; legacy records expose earlier inference history as unknown. The feature test and all required exact-head checks passed at merge commit `51bf3db79fdadca26dbde6bbf752c9982c7793e3`; post-merge CI passed on that merge commit. This is not yet a ceiling on all model requests: context-findings extraction and LLM compaction summary/requirement-probe calls do not reserve from it, nor do delegated `spawn_subagent` requests or durable operation work units. Without durable objective state, the counter comes from `ExecutionTracker.fresh()` and resets per turn. `NEXUS_MAX_INFERENCE_SUBMISSIONS` may be selected in a project `.env` up to 10,000; on resume, an existing saved ceiling can only be lowered. Availability probes are excluded. **P10-02 remains incomplete.** Remaining scope: include all side and delegated requests in a shared durable pre-dispatch counter; account for durable operation units, tokens and cumulative elapsed limits; add per-tool deadlines and process-crash reconciliation.\n52-\n53:**Follow-up progress (2026-11-26, merged PR #857):** Each `runTurn` installs an `AsyncLocalStorage` inference-admission scope. `client.ts` consults every active scope before each provider completion request, while the durable objective reserves and persists its counter before dispatch. Nested in-process turns inherit the enclosing scope, so provider retries/failovers, same-turn side requests, and nested delegates consume the same objective allowance; persistence failure prevents the provider call. `cli/test/p10_inference_submission_budget.test.ts` covers finite limits and overrides, retry accounting and resume, side requests and nested delegates, persisted parent allowance, failed persistence, and legacy unknown history. Required CI run `45739706503` and CodeQL run `45739706508` passed on merge commit `d5e3f602c721fdaf213d1031d1616364c126bf43` (PR #857). This does not create a shared counter across distinct durable operation units; their request counts remain separate. **WP-P10-02 remains incomplete** for operation-wide inference-request accounting and arbitrary side-effect recovery after process or host failure.\n54-\n55-**Progress (2026-11-24, merged PR #762):** `runTurnImpl` arms a timer for the active turn's `maxTurnMinutes` deadline and requests cancellation while inference or a tool is still awaiting completion. Expiry returns `LIMIT_REACHED` / `TIME_BUDGET_EXHAUSTED`; the timer is cleared on normal completion and caught failure. Tests cover aborting slow streamed inference, stopping a running shell command before its later side effect, and clearing the timer after a normal turn. Exact-head CI passed on PR #762 (`45293148069`); post-merge CI passed at `6b916195d57ddd4b641214ea3e835f10eb722a28` (`45294542653`). This bounded one active direct turn; PR #774 later added cumulative elapsed accounting across resumes of that durable objective. A separate per-tool deadline is still absent. **P10-02 remains incomplete.** Remaining scope includes shared durable accounting for side/delegated model requests and durable operation units, operation-wide elapsed limits, per-tool deadlines, and process-crash reconciliation.\n56-\n57:**Progress (2026-11-24, merged PR #774):** The direct durable-objective elapsed-time ceiling is configurable with `--max-task-minutes` / `NEXUS_MAX_TASK_MINUTES` (default and hard maximum 4,320 minutes). Active intervals are checkpointed before inference or tools; resume charges an unclosed interval, cannot raise its saved ceiling, and legacy records conservatively estimate from `objective.startedAt` or fail closed when that timestamp is unavailable. A backward wall-clock change exhausts the remaining budget rather than restoring it. The additive elapsed fields are included in the `run --json` contract without changing its schema version. PR #774's required checks passed at head `02e1d7550ea48bb91cbf5ba0c67a69deb88d4baf`; it merged as `45520edcf33569802698f58c43277cad1e04a0a4`. Post-merge CI run `45374543474` and CodeQL run `45374543479` completed successfully on that merge commit, which is an ancestor of the current release baseline `257d324d61bbd1957a464f5ca6312d49d18e1c68`. This ceiling applies to one durable objective ledger; it does not cap aggregate elapsed work across all units in a durable operation. **WP-P10-02 remains incomplete:** shared accounting for side/delegated requests, operation-wide elapsed limits, per-tool deadlines, and process-crash/unknown-outcome reconciliation remain.\n58-\n59:**Progress (2026-11-25, implementation in progress):** The durable operation runner now has a finite aggregate active wall-time cap (default and hard maximum 4,320 minutes) shared across concurrently executing units. Each active interval is recorded in the operation record; an open interval is charged on recovery, and migrated records conservatively use record age because earlier active time cannot be reconstructed. Expiry aborts the current operation signal, pauses the operation, and records interrupted side effects as unknown so resume cannot silently replay them. The existing `nexus operation budget` command now audits token and active-time cap changes together, and operation status/JSON reports elapsed, remaining, and uncertain legacy history. The implementation deliberately measures the operation driver's wall interval, not the sum of worker CPU time or time spent parked between runs. Focused evidence on the current branch: `p10_operation_active_budget.test.ts` (5/5), `goal_pa3_completion.test.ts` (8/8), `operation_runner_refactor.test.ts` (3/3), `operation_schema.test.ts` (19/19), `operation_view.test.ts` (35/35), and `npm run build` passed. This remains one tranche, not complete WP-P10-02: side/delegated inference requests, token accounting across all operation work, per-tool deadlines, process crash during arbitrary side effects, and durable unknown-outcome reconciliation beyond the in-flight budget-expiry boundary remain open. Exact-head hosted checks and post-merge CI have not yet run for this change.\n60-\n61-**Progress (2026-11-26, RPC worker timeout tranche, merged PR #862):** Generic Rust RPC deadlines and cancellation now retire the worker process rather than only ending the bridge wait. A retiring worker accepts no new dispatch; sibling in-flight calls resolve as outcome unknown; stdin EOF requests the Rust worker's normal cleanup; and a bounded grace period ends a worker that does not exit. Command cancellation retains its runner confirmation window before retirement. Focused evidence: `rpc_worker_timeout.test.ts` covers unanswered RPC, sibling unknown outcomes, cancellation, bounded forced exit, and a blocked real Rust filesystem RPC followed by clean worker replacement; `worker_stdin_epipe.test.ts` guards existing disconnect handling; `npm --prefix cli run build` passed. Required PR checks and CodeQL passed at exact head `8ea727e878efea7750e811c8836542299e704e30`; post-merge CI run `45760041918` and CodeQL run `45760041919` passed on release merge `1c48520411d3ddff2dbe8f2bb33cc922394e5e7d`. This closes the unowned in-flight worker portion of per-tool deadlines, but does not add configurable per-tool deadlines, guarantee cancellation of kernel-blocked I/O, or resolve arbitrary side-effect outcomes after process termination.\n62-\n63:**Progress (2026-11-26, tool timeout hard bound):** `NEXUS_MAX_TOOL_TIMEOUT_SECS` is now parsed as a strict positive integer and capped at 86,400 seconds. This keeps the command runner's outer RPC timer within Node's supported timer range. Before the cap, `NEXUS_MAX_TOOL_TIMEOUT_SECS=2147484` produced a `2,147,489,000ms` bridge timer; a direct Node probe showed that an oversized timer fires after 1ms, which could prematurely retire the worker and return an unknown result. The regression test verifies oversized and malformed operator values and that the bridge timeout remains below `2,147,483,647ms`. This initially bounded the command timeout path; the non-command bridge input path is addressed by the follow-up below. WP-P10-02 remains incomplete for aggregate inference-request accounting across durable work units and arbitrary side-effect recovery.\n64-\n65:**Follow-up progress (2026-11-26, non-command RPC timeout input bound):** `rpcDeadlines` now accepts a non-command `timeout_secs` only when it is a positive safe integer, caps it using the existing `maxToolTimeoutSecs()` operator limit, and otherwise uses the finite 35-second RPC default. This also prevents untrusted bridge inputs from overflowing Node timers. The regression was reproduced before the fix: `Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER` yielded a `9,007,199,254,740,996,000ms` bridge delay. The test covers the hard cap, a lower operator override, negative/fractional/non-finite/unsafe inputs, and default fallback. Local evidence on Node 22.23.5 and 24.11.1: focused timeout, process-bound and RPC-worker suites passed 15/15 on each runtime; `make build`, `make lint`, and `git diff --check` passed. This closes timeout arithmetic for the covered bridge paths, but does not resolve aggregate inference-request accounting across durable work units or arbitrary side-effect recovery; WP-P10-02 remains incomplete.\n66-\n67:**Follow-up progress (2026-11-26, merged PR #869):** Durable operation state now stores a finite `maxInferenceCalls` policy (default 1,000; hard maximum 10,000), the consumed `inferenceCalls` count, and whether historical usage is known. Each provider submission made under an operation work-unit execution scope reserves and persists one count before dispatch; persistence failure prevents the request. Older records migrate with unknown history and pause before dispatch until the operator explicitly starts an accounting epoch. Reaching the cap pauses the operation, and changing the cap preserves the consumed count. The existing `nexus operation budget` command configures or explicitly resets this accounting and reports usage. The five tests in `p10_operation_inference_budget.test.ts` cover limits, cross-unit counting and exhaustion, cap changes and legacy reset, unknown legacy history, and failed reservation persistence. Local evidence: the CLI build passed and the focused operation origin, semantic conflict, and team convergence suites passed 62/62. Required Node 22/24 matrix, Linux, and CodeQL checks passed on the exact PR merge ref `ae1b0d560153985b2c94a9d095ce5ecbcab11d51`; PR #869 merged as `50207a6781ee1152950f0d42a3087aee034cc790`. Post-merge CI run `45807999265` and CodeQL run `45807999264` both passed on that release merge. This closes aggregate inference submission accounting across durable operation work units, but does not establish one counter across every possible provider call path or resolve arbitrary side-effect outcomes after process or host failure. **WP-P10-02 remains incomplete.**\n68-\n69-**Follow-up progress (2026-11-26, merged PR #877):** `collectOperationUsage` provides the shared read-only rollup of child-task token records for operation admission, goal accounting, operation status and budget views. It separates reported from estimated tokens and marks missing, malformed, and in-progress usage incomplete instead of presenting it as exact. Operation view schema v4 adds `tokenUsage`. PR #877 merged as `443f52b6871fedf4d65f0a99d10b5c41cdc91d7c`; post-merge CI run `45877783322` and CodeQL run `45877783393` passed on that commit. This rollup makes accounting and reporting consistent but is not a hard per-request token ceiling.\n70-\n71-**Follow-up progress (2026-11-26, merged PR #881):** Before an operation can become terminal `COMPLETE`, `OperationRunner` rechecks the persisted token rollup when `maxTotalTokens` is configured. It pauses on a measured overrun or incomplete/unavailable usage, while allowing exact-cap completion when no further work needs dispatch; the completed unit remains available for operator review. The focused token-reporting, inference-budget, and goal-lifecycle tests passed 21/21. PR #881 merged as `5061a3e83ce6daf34f34785bd3753f11604ba77d`; post-merge CI run `45896185332` and CodeQL run `45896185330` passed. This prevents false terminal success after the last batch but does not stop a dispatched request or concurrent batch from exceeding the configured token cap.\n72-\n73-**Follow-up progress (2026-11-26, merged PR #883):** Operation schema v10 persists cumulative request-token estimates. The existing async-local admission hook carries an estimate based on serialized input length plus the configured output allowance; operation scope reserves it with the inference submission count before dispatch. Concurrent calls cannot reserve the same remaining estimate, reservations persist across resume and are not refunded after cancellation or unknown outcomes, and reservation failure prevents dispatch. The final completion gate still checks persisted child usage. Generated ACP, run-result, and sidecar schemas/ledgers were updated. Focused reservation, migration, semantic-conflict tests passed 17/17, and protocol contract tests passed 30/30. PR #883 merged as `09f28d7e73633716859d1c1bb1edd30f26b5a7c6`; post-merge CI run `45908696882` and CodeQL run `45908696908` passed.\n74-\n75-**Follow-up progress (2026-11-27, merged PR #885):** The request admission scope now narrows the provider output allowance to the remaining operation estimate and serializes the limit for OpenAI Chat, OpenAI Responses, Anthropic, Gemini, and Bedrock. If a provider rejects the required limit, or the active protocol cannot represent it, the request is not retried without the cap. The prompt-side token estimate remains heuristic, and Nexus cannot prove that each remote provider honors a serialized limit; this is not an exact or hard provider-spend ceiling. The focused provider/operation set passed 160/160, with build and lint passing. PR #885 merged as `d8d2fe2ab8698286660db8d9d78d4f2d5aedc74a`; post-merge CI run `45917052347` and CodeQL run `45917052388` passed.\n76-\n77-**Process-crash recovery evidence (2026-11-27, merged PR #887):** A separate Nexus process using a local fake gateway is killed after `run_command` has written a side-effect marker but before its result is saved. Resume reports the interrupted effect as unknown and refuses to replay it; the marker remains at one. The test passed on Node 22 and 24. PR #887 merged as `9bfe2ed86bfcf092e0406e71b83cb5b3e7e2ce62`; post-merge CI run `45927066566` and CodeQL run `45927066332` passed.\n78-\n79-**Follow-up process-crash sibling matrix (PR #894):** The real-process regression also covers an ordered two-call response: the first `run_command` completes and is persisted; a second writes an effect and remains in flight when Nexus is killed. Resume retains the first result, reports the second as unknown, and does not repeat either effect. The focused test passed 2/2. PR #894 merged as `5509e1f59ccced4f4e470cdd2ba5006bd73dcd77`; post-merge CI run `45947491306` and CodeQL run `45947491736` passed.\n80-\n81-**Provider-request crash accounting (PR #895):** With a local fake gateway holding a provider response after receipt, the test kills Nexus and resumes with a higher configured limit; the saved request count remains 1/1 and Nexus sends no second request. The focused test passed on Node 22 and 24. PR #895 merged as `91e9633cbaee9f6a9dca00cacf94f50e25c1b97c`; post-merge CI run `45951101344` and CodeQL run `45951101366` passed.\n82-\n83-**Provider-outcome recovery (PR #901):** Each provider request in objective and durable-operation scopes receives a persisted reservation before dispatch, then settles that specific reservation when Nexus observes a response. On resume, leftover in-flight requests are recorded as unknown before further work; legacy migrations do not invent prior outcomes. This replaces the earlier provider-crash test's weaker “budget exhausted, so no retry” evidence with an explicit unknown-outcome record. The request stays charged; this does not establish provider completion, billing, or exactly-once execution. Required checks passed at PR head `d0053ae4f7b78a3c23d26caf376665184f4020c9`; PR #901 merged as `af10e7b93c2bf2142cd738bda38aa575698c5813`, with post-merge CI run `46001827052` and CodeQL run `46001826978` passing on that commit.\n84-\n85-**Durable mutation-intent failure gate (PR #924, merged as `d4feb4d925d22f8d5517d306f5b13df8e8eec2a9`):** A fault-injected failure of `recordOperationStart` previously still allowed `write_file` to execute and create its target. Durable mutating tool calls now return a failed, not-executed receipt when the start record cannot be persisted (including when the durable state manager is unavailable); they do not capture a baseline or dispatch the handler, and they do not append an unmatched end record. If the current task record has not yet been materialized, the existing durable checkpoint path writes it before the operation start; a checkpoint failure also blocks dispatch. The `p10_operation_journal_fail_closed.test.ts` regression exercises a real fake-provider request and Rust file-write handler with start-journal persistence forced to fail. This closes the observed start-record gap for durable registered mutating tools. It does not cover transient non-durable subagent calls, prove host/power-loss behavior, or change the existing degraded-recovery treatment when an end-record write fails. Local verification included the focused regression, `npm run build`, `make lint`, and `git diff --check`. The first PR commit failed the hosted critical-complexity gate because `executeToolCall` increased from CC 43 to 51; helpers reduced it to CC 30. The first exact-head hosted run exposed test fixtures that replaced the constructor's durable store without materializing the task record; checkpoint-before-journal now handles those supported lazy-checkpoint entry paths. All exact-head required CI checks passed on `a06a00d01895b11aaec4de2227b8eff5e3586ec4` (CI run `46089209876`, CodeQL run `46089209866`); advisory Entire Gates failed and did not block the merge under repository policy. Post-merge CI run `46090864177` and CodeQL run `46090864175` passed on `d4feb4d925d22f8d5517d306f5b13df8e8eec2a9`.\n86-\n87-**Per-tool Rust RPC deadline slice (PR #922):** `NEXUS_TOOL_TIMEOUT_SECS_<TOOL>` sets a Rust RPC method's default timeout and ceiling; request-level values can shorten it, while `NEXUS_MAX_TOOL_TIMEOUT_SECS` remains the global hard cap. `run_command` receives the effective value in its runner request and keeps the longer bridge grace; a timed-out non-command RPC retires its shared worker and reports pending outcomes as unknown. Local `make build`, `make lint`, and `git diff --check` passed. On Node 22.22.2 and 24.11.1, `verifier_timeout.test.ts` passed 8/8, `rpc_worker_timeout.test.ts` 6/6, `process_bounds_and_services.test.ts` 3/3, and `tool_surface_characterization.test.ts` 6/6. PR #922 merged as `ed55744f8fda33dcc6410a664e9e50bd62d7d6f3`; required checks passed on the exact merge candidate `83a17fe8f2c3dce3e102b572c7ab8e18d144dce5`, and post-merge CI run `46078951080` plus CodeQL run `46078951108` passed on the release merge commit.\n88-\n89-**Provider request deadline check (release head `a1f07419ba63402eb5f01dfe85783ae282aa9817`):** `LocalLlmClient.createWatchdog` applies a finite whole-request deadline to inference requests (900,000 ms default; 3,600,000 ms hard maximum) in addition to first-byte, idle, and caller-cancellation signals (`cli/src/client.ts:361-369,1154-1175`). On Node 24.11.1, `provider_robustness.test.ts` and `inference_transport.test.ts` passed 46/46, including an always-trickling stream stopped by the total deadline, a silent-server timeout, and operator cancellation. This demonstrates a local client request deadline for the exercised inference paths. It does not prove a remote provider stops processing or billing after local cancellation, and it is not an exact provider-spend ceiling.\n90-\n91-**Plugin execution boundary (closed by PR #932):** The pre-fix findings above showed that asynchronous TypeScript plugin hooks could outlive their caller and that late middleware continuation could dispatch a tool after timeout. PR #932 (`3394b3a429d806256ab07936d62ed0586eaec907`, merged as `d0276535118aa197fc56371ab697220e20d5fd43`) moves file-loaded plugin callbacks into persistent, killable worker processes using versioned JSON IPC, a 1 MiB message cap, a 64 KiB combined stdout/stderr cap, and finite per-callback deadlines (default 15 seconds, maximum 60 seconds). Middleware continuation remains parent-owned; malformed or late messages fail closed; possibly-started custom tools and continuations report unknown outcomes; cancellation and shutdown retire workers. Workspace plugins run from a private temporary copy of the exact approved bytes. Local `make build`, `npm run build`, and `npm run typecheck` passed; the 82-test plugin/trust/policy compatibility set passed on Node 24.11.1, and focused containment tests passed 13/13 on both Node 22.22.2 and Node 24.11.1. Adversarial coverage includes synchronous infinite loops, never-settling callbacks, late continuation, timeout after continuation, cancellation, malformed/oversized IPC, and ordinary/detached child cleanup. Required PR checks passed at the exact head (Node 22/24 gates and matrices, all eight test shards, Linux packaging, and CodeQL); post-merge CI run `46107372969` and CodeQL run `46107372886` passed on merge commit `d0276535118aa197fc56371ab697220e20d5fd43`. This is execution containment, not an OS security sandbox or CPU/memory quota. Windows/macOS process-tree cleanup, host-crash cleanup, aggregate resource limits, and same-user deliberate protocol manipulation remain unproven.\n92-\n93:**WP-P10-02 status and remaining limits:** Merged changes provide operation-wide inference-submission reservations, estimated-token admission, provider-serialized output limits, a terminal measured-usage gate, persisted unknown provider outcomes after process interruption, process-crash evidence for selected command/provider paths, and bounded plugin callback execution from PR #932. PR #922 adds per-tool deadline defaults/caps for Rust RPC calls. Local provider inference requests also have a finite whole-request client deadline, with focused Node 24 tests passing 46/46 as recorded above; remote provider processing/billing after local abort remains unknown. These controls do not demonstrate exact token usage or provider spend: input-token admission is heuristic and remote providers may ignore a serialized output cap. Plugin process containment does not impose aggregate CPU/memory quotas or establish host-crash cleanup; crash tests do not establish host/power-loss safety or unknown-outcome recovery for every side-effectful tool/provider path. **WP-P10-02 remains incomplete** for exact/provider-enforced cumulative token containment and recovery qualification for other side-effect classes and host/power failure.\n94-\n95-## WP-P10-03 — Acceptance evidence integrity and export\n96-\n97-**Priority:** P1 · **Complexity:** M · **Dependencies:** none for local evidence integrity; independent policy identity remains unavailable under WP-P10-01.\n98-\n99-**Existing capability:** `FlightRecorder` hash-chains persisted events and `FlightPlayer` reports altered, reordered, removed or unchained records (`cli/src/core/hash_chain.ts`, `cli/src/flight.ts`). GV-2 already exports bounded audit bundles with per-file SHA-256, a canonical manifest, hash-linked file entries, offline verification, and optional operator-key Ed25519 signatures (`cli/src/core/audit_bundle.ts`, `cli/src/audit_contract.ts`). `CandidateTracker` invalidates receipts on known mutations and recomputes the candidate identity before merge (`cli/src/core/candidate.ts`). These controls must be reused; this package does not need a second chain or evidence store.\n100-\n101-**Confirmed gap:** Before this slice, `FlightRecorder.appendEvent` silently returned an apparent event after a write or redaction failure while leaving later writes on the same chain (`cli/src/flight.ts`). A later complete chain could therefore omit a middle event without the on-disk chain indicating missing evidence. Flight logs are optional telemetry and must not block authorized work, but they must not continue to attest to an intact chain after a skipped event. The completion receipt also does not yet carry a complete, durable environment and acceptance-policy identity; distinguish that remaining work from the existing candidate binding and audit-bundle integrity.\n102-\n103-**Affected surfaces:** `flight.ts`, `evidence.ts`, completion oracle, durable state and audit commands.\n104-\n105-**Design / reuse:** Keep the GV-3 flight chain and GV-2 audit bundle. On any event omission, stop chaining subsequent events so the existing verifier reports a broken or unchained log; return an explicit `recordingSkipped` result without blocking the authorized action. For later work, extend the existing receipt/bundle only for missing candidate, verifier, environment and policy identity. Use operator-supplied signing only when explicitly configured; unsigned bundles remain clearly unsigned. Do not promote optional flight telemetry into a mandatory acceptance store.\n106-\n107-**Implementation steps:**\n108-\n109-1. Preserve the existing canonical hash-chain and versioned audit-bundle formats.\n110-2. Mark the current flight chain unverifiable when sanitization or append failure skips a record; retain the caller-visible failure result and keep telemetry non-blocking.\n111-3. Extend the existing persisted validation receipt with source revision and candidate digest/id, verifier definition digest, command/result, runtime identity, and acceptance-policy digest; retain existing bounded candidate-binding export and legacy-receipt compatibility.\n112-4. Keep redaction before hashing/export and retain current artifact limits and retention behavior.\n113-5. Keep acceptance-critical receipt persistence distinct from optional flight telemetry; write failures in mandatory acceptance evidence must remain non-success.\n114-\n115-**Progress (2026-11-23):** The flight recorder now returns `recordingSkipped` and disables further chaining after sanitization or append failure. A failure-injection test reproduces an `EISDIR` append failure and confirms the next persisted event makes the flight read as `CHAIN BROKEN`; a separate test covers sanitizer failure. This intentionally does not block tool execution.\n116-\n117-**Follow-up progress (2026-11-24, merged PR #763):** Sanitization and append failures now attempt to persist a payload-free, unchained `recording_gap` marker, retain degraded status across recorder restart, and emit one operator warning per recorder. If the filesystem also rejects the marker write, only the runtime warning survives; persistence cannot be guaranteed when the evidence sink is unavailable. `nexus replay` reports skipped evidence explicitly, including when the first record was skipped. Audit verification returns a finding and nonzero result when a bundled log was already broken at export; an unchained legacy log remains a stated limitation. The PR's required checks passed at head `a18ec4c991fa583b52ba6ead441e9afe7d5df36c`; post-merge CI passed on `c2faaa89922a21e90bb1916c654015d417b9b582` (run `45307469727`).\n118-\n119-**Follow-up progress (2026-11-24, merged PR #764):** `nexus audit verify --json` now fails closed when the flight log contains a durable `recording_gap` marker, including when the marker is the first record and the remaining log is unchained. The verifier reports `tampered` with `log_recording_gap_at_export`; clean bundles retain their prior result. The limit when disk failure prevents the marker from being persisted is documented. Required checks passed at head `5e5bb11f72630c16cad54b71456b512e9a20f370`; post-merge CI passed on `b74edef04083fcdcb2a53b842790792a174e3bcc` (run `45309777194`).\n120-\n121-**Candidate-binding slice (PR #771, merged as `3b15d89fd421b23e1161cae577f6e7c373236312`):** `nexus audit export` adds a bounded `receipts/candidate-binding.json` report, reusing the existing candidate identity routine and the bundle's existing `validation_receipts` artefact role. It records match, mismatch, partial, no-receipt, or unavailable outcomes; the human-readable export prints the status, and it never upgrades a verifier result or acceptance decision. The candidate scan is capped at 50,000 entries and 256 MiB; binding output is capped at 10,000 receipts and explicitly describes its non-atomic sampling relationship to bundle collection. Platforms where the existing safe candidate traversal cannot run report unavailable. The added `git ls-files` invocation disables `core.fsmonitor`, preventing a repository-configured monitor from running if local Git configuration changes after the audit-export preflight. Local verification passed: `audit_export.test.ts` 19/19, `candidate_git_paths.test.ts` 36/36, and the TypeScript build. Required checks passed on PR merge candidate `117ea0a807c0070333d401da5f7994cb694cc705`; post-merge CI passed on `3b15d89fd421b23e1161cae577f6e7c373236312`.\n122-\n123-**Stacked Git-configuration follow-up (PR #772, merged as `fb396a42ac311670e52dd4bf3153b2e464ac3a84`):** Audit export lists repository-scoped Git settings that name executable programs or redirect Git behavior and marks receipt binding unavailable when any are present or the listing fails. Its five adversarial configurations are tested with a same-size tracked-file re-hash. The negative control without the preflight executes the planted clean filter and fails the marker assertion. Local verification passed on Node 24: `audit_export` 20/20, `audit_command` 8/8, `audit_bundle` 25/25, `audit_schema` 7/7, `leak_guard` 6/6, `candidate_git_paths` 36/36, and `tsc --noEmit`. Required PR checks passed on merge candidate `99a742ce529df0b79facb936002993e46e1d16f6`; post-merge CI passed on `fb396a42ac311670e52dd4bf3153b2e464ac3a84` (run `45351066175`; CodeQL run `45351065784`). The non-required Entire check failed and did not gate merging under repository guidance.\n124-\n125-**Mandatory receipt-persistence failure slice (PR #858, merged as `33565128d7e2be14d7e80a09757144c3da28430a`):** If durable candidate-receipt persistence fails or the durable task state is unavailable, the in-memory receipt is invalidated, verified completion remains false, and the turn returns `PERSISTENCE_FAILED`; the speculative worktree is retained and cannot auto-merge. Failure injection verifies this path, while the successful receipt-binding regression still verifies normal acceptance and merge. Local focused tests passed (2/2), adjacent audit-export tests passed (21/21), and `npm run build` passed. Required checks passed on the exact PR merge candidate; post-merge CI run `45745010423` and CodeQL run `45745010340` passed on descendant release tip `1a5a05b204bba6cebe4ccbf2aaf94c571d474eae`, which includes the fix.\n126-\n127-**Acceptance identity slice (PR #863, merged):** Candidate validation receipts now include the source revision and candidate digest/id, verifier command and result, a digest of the pre-change verifier definition when available, Nexus/Node/OS/architecture identity, and a canonical digest of the effective policy and verifier configuration. Raw verifier contents and effective policy are not copied into the receipt. The receipt explicitly records `independentApproval: \"unavailable_single_maintainer\"`; it does not assert independent review. `p10_receipt_persistence.test.ts` checks the receipt fields, confirms the verifier source sentinel is absent, and retains the failure-closed persistence assertions. `audit_export.test.ts` checks that the existing state manager and audit bundle preserve the identity. Required PR checks passed on exact head `2d10d3780c0621d3cd5b9aecb811cbe30b4ea074`; post-merge CI run `45773723629` and CodeQL run `45773723883` passed on release commit `a421799bacaeb94d5dc64b5726dce18e6c1dd658`. WP-P10-03 still has same-user evidence mutability and no independent approval.\n128-\n129-**Legacy receipt export follow-up (PR #867, merged as `f5e88e4f39ef5bfed8b674738575a9522b37f3ec`):** The existing `receipts/candidate-binding.json` now records per sampled receipt whether a structured acceptance identity object was saved, plus the count of sampled legacy receipts without one. Export warnings and human-readable output call out that absence. This is a presence report only: it does not validate the identity object's claims, alter the historical receipt status, or indicate verifier success or acceptance. The regression `legacy validation receipts remain readable and are explicitly reported without structured acceptance identity` verifies an old `PASSED` receipt is exported without synthesizing identity and the task store remains byte-for-byte unchanged. On Node 24, the full `audit_export.test.ts` file passed 22/22 with `TMPDIR=/var/tmp` to avoid the host's unrelated `/tmp/.git` marker; `npm --prefix cli run build` and `git diff --check` passed. Required exact-head checks passed on `396c4a5e3be7174453f5c3e9e4ba43d714a37b85` (CI run `45785980552`, CodeQL run `45785980671`); post-merge CI run `45788238436` and CodeQL run `45788238467` passed on merge commit `f5e88e4f39ef5bfed8b674738575a9522b37f3ec`. Local bundles and their source stores remain mutable by the same user.\n130-\n131-**Receipt-construction fail-closed slice (PR #884, release; PR #886, main):** The first implementation's outer receipt-construction catch only cleared `verifiedWorktreeCompletion`; if candidate identity construction threw after a passing configured verifier, `task_completed` still returned success and the final turn was `COMPLETED/VERIFIED`. A failure-injection regression reproduced this at release base `abfa58c62c651ed4e113d0d9203667986739289d`. The catch now sets the existing acceptance failure latch, clears the active in-memory receipt, and invalidates any receipt for the candidate worktree. `run_turn.ts` consumes that latch as `PERSISTENCE_FAILED`; no change to the turn runner was needed. The merged regression asserts non-success, no active receipt, and no auto-merge while retaining the worktree. PR #884 merged as `4987ac573fe8415e3fe561a2adbb82d77014afde` on `release/v1.3.1`; all required exact-head checks passed, and post-merge CI run `45911392549` and CodeQL run `45911392550` succeeded on that merge commit. PR #886 forward-ported the fix to `main` at `881798b282a0bc1198628955d5645ae27402b1b6`; all required exact-head checks passed (CI run `45920208046`, CodeQL run `45920208028`). I reran `p10_receipt_persistence.test.ts` and `p10_process_crash_recovery.test.ts` together on Node 24.11.1 with the worktree-local Rust core built; all 3 tests passed. The earlier local attempts without that core were invalid because the verifier/tool runner never reached receipt creation. No independent approver is available; this local failure-injection test does not add external evidence immutability or independent review.\n132-\n133-**Required tests / acceptance:** Existing tests cover altered, deleted, reordered and truncated events and candidate mutation invalidation. Failure injection confirms an omitted event cannot be followed by an apparently intact chain, sanitizer failure does not preserve that claim, and mandatory receipt-persistence failure prevents verified acceptance. The release/v1.3.1 acceptance-identity slice adds source revision, candidate digest, verifier definition digest, command/result, runtime, and policy identity to durable receipts and verifies bundle retention. Legacy receipt export migration behavior is now tested: older receipts remain readable with their historical status, are not assigned synthesized acceptance identity, and are explicitly identified as missing structured identity. Exact-head CI and post-merge CI/CodeQL are complete for the legacy export follow-up. Same-user bundle mutability and the lack of independent approval remain explicit limitations; local evidence does not provide external immutability or a second-person review.\n134-\n135-**Migration / rollback:** Legacy logs remain readable but classify `integrity=unverified_legacy`; never silently “upgrade” them to verified. Keep old export path read-only during migration. Residual risk: same-user can delete both evidence and local chain unless an external signed/immutable copy is retained.\n136-\n137:## WP-P10-04 — Resource admission and cleanup envelope\n138-\n139:**Priority:** P1 · **Complexity:** M/L · **Dependencies:** WP-P10-02 for shared task budgets; existing operation-local scheduling is already implemented.\n140-\n141-**Gap/evidence:** The earlier claim that `Promise.all` makes operation scheduling unbounded was inaccurate. `ConvergenceScheduler.assign` bounds in-flight units by `effectiveConcurrency`, resource slots, and operation-local CPU/memory reservations (`cli/src/core/scheduler.ts:267-307`); parallel execution defaults to one and requires explicit per-unit requests and a per-operation budget (`cli/src/core/operation_schema.ts:101-119`). `OperationRunner.executeBatch` uses `Promise.all` only for the already-admitted due set (`cli/src/core/operation_runner.ts:276-299`). PR #830 added same-host cross-operation slots for declared inference resources; PR #831 added same-home LSP process slots; the active-client slice below bounds ordinary Nexus client processes per OS user. Remaining gaps are aggregate CPU/memory consumption, session counts hosted inside one client process, write-time disk limits outside session-history archives, provider request/token/cost totals across separate processes, and cleanup/recovery accounting outside the existing lease boundaries. Those current gaps are not evidence that per-operation scheduling is unbounded, and no OS-level host CPU/memory ceiling is claimed.\n142-\n143:**Follow-up evidence (2026-11-26, current release):** Cross-operation inference-resource slots and same-home LSP slots have since been implemented in `SharedResourceAdmission` (PRs #830 and #831; see `docs/assurance/WP-P10-04_RESOURCE_ADMISSION_BASELINE.md`). A separate process-boundary probe on release commit `5061a3e83ce6daf34f34785bd3753f11604ba77d` confirms that `UsageLedger` provider request/token/cost caps remain process-local: two fresh Node 24.11.1 processes sharing one temporary `HOME` and workspace each admitted under `NEXUS_CLOUD_MAX_REQUESTS=1`, then wrote two receipts to the shared workspace file. No live inference was run. This is a confirmed gap only if these limits are expected to aggregate across processes; their reset/window semantics are not defined, so do not turn them into persistent global quotas without an explicit accounting epoch. Durable operation inference counters are the existing operation-scoped control and must not be duplicated here.\n144-\n145-**Affected surfaces:** `core/resources.ts`, operation scheduler/runner, client leases, tool runner, durable state/compaction and process cleanup.\n146-\n147-**Design / reuse:** Preserve the existing operation-local scheduler and `ResourceRegistry`; do not duplicate their admission checks. First inventory actual session, process, storage and provider ownership. Extend an existing shared lifecycle/lease boundary only where it can enforce aggregate limits across operations and sessions; if no such boundary exists, specify the smallest shared admission interface and make its ownership explicit. Reuse Rust process registry, output ring buffer, sandbox pids limit, `client_lease`, per-operation lock and session archival where they already cover the resource. Do not claim provider spend limits without a reliable token/cost source; use bounded request/token accounting when available and describe provider-side uncertainty.\n148-\n149-**Required tests / acceptance:** Existing operation-level tests continue to show that units beyond `maxConcurrentUnits` or explicit CPU/memory budget remain unlaunched. New multi-operation/session tests demonstrate that aggregate configured limits are not exceeded under concurrent admission and that rejected admission has no side effect. Cancellation, process exit, failed persistence, and restart release or reconcile leases without orphan children. Repeated compaction/resume respects a measured active-context bound; retained disk has deterministic quota/retention behavior. Cross-session tests demonstrate no cross-workspace data access. Each claim must identify whether the limit is enforced in-process, across processes, or only per operation.\n150-\n151-**Migration / rollback:** Start with advisory resource estimates, then enforce with operator-visible defaults; do not kill unrelated sessions. Rollback disables only the new admission layer while retaining process cleanup. Residual risk: OS estimates are approximate; host OOM remains an external failure mode without cgroups/container limits.\n152-\n153:**Progress (2026-11-27, merged PR #889):** A shared, cross-process active-operation cap now reuses `SharedResourceAdmission.acquireNamed`; it does not add a parallel admission framework. `NEXUS_MAX_SHARED_OPERATION_UNITS` defaults to 4 and accepts operator values from 1 through 64. The aggregate lease is acquired before the provider lease, released if provider admission misses, and held through executor completion and result persistence. Focused tests passed 33/33 and the TypeScript build passed on Node 24.11.1. Required PR checks passed at head `f552c2c885b7fe9d00b9acd1677435f084d20be6` and merge candidate `f07cf89d0e44885dbb24beef6721ac7540212ea5`. Post-merge CI run `45932311657` and CodeQL run `45932311572` passed on merge `0390df6b5ff619201600360ff016364ca0bc6af6`; Entire remained advisory. **WP-P10-04 remains partially open:** this control limits active operation units across cooperating current-version processes using the same admission store. It does not bound aggregate CPU/memory, session count, disk use, provider spend, or processes that do not participate in this lease protocol; older running Nexus versions do not hold the new lease until they exit. Cancellation and crash reconciliation outside the existing lease boundary still need separate evidence.\n154-\n155:**Progress (2026-11-27, merged PR #919):** Session history archive writes enforce an aggregate cap equal to the existing 2 GiB retained-state ceiling. `SessionManager.assertSessionArchiveCapacity` scans archive files under the existing state-store guard, rejects unreadable/non-regular entries and scans above 100,000 entries, and refuses a write before archive append or fork creation if it would cross the cap. It preserves existing archives and does not add another cleanup or admission framework. The new `session_archive_bounds.test.ts` covers aggregate accounting across sessions, byte preservation on refusal, fork refusal before state creation, inability to raise the ceiling through options, and empty append behavior at a full quota. With the worktree-local Rust core built, `session_archive_bounds.test.ts`, `session_fork.test.ts`, and `compaction_continuity.test.ts` passed **43/43** on Node 24.11.1 (exit 0); process-death recovery cases and the existing archive-write failure case were included. After the final empty-append guard, the current archive and fork suites passed **10/10**, `npm --prefix cli run build` passed, and `git diff --check` passed. PR #919 merged at `2026-11-27T20:00:55Z` as `a944a4eac448fdd1b08f1adfdca5be5696376ad2`. Required PR-head CI run `46059219684` and CodeQL run `46059219707` passed at exact PR head `d1778581428312574f24057e5b2741e6d3aae802`. Post-merge CI run `46060674463` passed both Node test gates, all eight shards, Linux packaging, and diff coverage; CodeQL run `46060674335` also passed on the exact merge commit. Entire Gates failed on the PR head but is advisory and excluded from required merge gates by project policy. Scope is limited to `*.history.jsonl` archives written by participating current-version Nexus processes; this is not a general disk quota and does not cap other state, external writers, or older running versions. **WP-P10-04 remains partially open** for the broader CPU/memory, session-count, provider-accounting, and cleanup/recovery gaps above.\n156-\n157:**Progress (2026-11-28, merged PR #934):** The normal CLI and sidecar lifecycle reserve a named slot through the existing `SharedResourceAdmission` before using state. Admission is scoped per canonical home and defaults to 64 concurrent client processes; the launching environment may lower `NEXUS_MAX_ACTIVE_CLIENTS` from 1 to 64 because admission runs before project `.env` loading. The kernel releases slots on process exit, and the existing exclusive restore lease remains unchanged. A 16-process same-home regression reproduced false capacity errors under zero-wait guard contention, then passed in three consecutive runs with the bounded 10-second guard wait; genuine slot exhaustion still fails closed. `npm run build` passed on Node 24.11.1. Required exact-head checks passed at PR head `4f55c6d4bfd4a58f480816b5871d5b97c36a0221`: both Node test matrices, all eight test shards, Linux build, JavaScript/TypeScript, and CodeQL. PR #934 merged at `2026-11-28T07:16:02Z` as `9b415dfdf8b754dd67a6cbb7b0a5084a1bfe9395`; post-merge CI run `46120407959` and CodeQL run `46120408066` passed on that exact merge commit. Entire Gates failed on the PR head, but is advisory and not a required merge check. This bounds cooperating Nexus CLI processes per canonical home on one Linux host, not in-process sessions, aggregate CPU/memory, separate homes, or non-Nexus processes. **WP-P10-04 remains partially open** for the other aggregate resource, provider-accounting, and cleanup/recovery gaps above.\n158-\n159-## WP-P10-05 — Compiler/static-analysis gate and hotspot metrics\n160-\n161-**Priority:** P2 · **Complexity:** S/M · **Dependencies:** none; independent protection of CI remains unavailable under WP-P10-01.\n162-\n163-**Gap/evidence:** Strict TS build, CodeQL JS/TS, Clippy, and diff-scoped rustfmt are present in CI. No Rust CodeQL or targeted complexity gate was found. `runTurnImpl` remains a critical large function.\n164-\n165-**Affected surfaces:** `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, `core/Cargo.toml`, TS configuration and targeted complexity documentation.\n166-\n167-**Design / reuse:** Reuse the existing `make lint` gate for typecheck, Clippy, and changed-file rustfmt; do not add a duplicate CI job. Keep Rust warnings scoped and justified rather than inventing a large analyzer bundle. Add complexity reporting or thresholds only for critical high-change functions after measuring the baseline; avoid arbitrary whole-repository size caps.\n168-\n169-**Progress (2026-11-23, `main` at `9156571f7d5fd8f8436c3105f0237628ab35e857`):** The Clippy CI gate proposed above is already implemented through `make lint` (`Makefile:24-27`), which runs CLI typecheck, Clippy with `-D warnings`, and diff-scoped rustfmt. CI calls this target in `test-matrix (22)`, `test-matrix (24)`, and `linux-x86_64` (`.github/workflows/ci.yml:74-75,196-197`). Workflow run `45047617708` completed successfully on that exact main SHA, including all three static-gate steps. Do not add a duplicate Clippy job. Remaining work in this package is a narrowly scoped, evidence-based complexity metric and its enforcement policy; the independent protection dependency from WP-P10-01 remains unavailable in this single-user repository.\n170-\n171-**Progress (2026-11-24, merged PR #766):** Measured the critical-path baseline at release commit `4d9996d485c2db866918c110d35e1c1a1d3b2911` using Babel's TypeScript parser. `runTurnImpl` is 1,262 lines / CC 324 / nesting 5; `dispatchToolCallImpl` is 900 / CC 288 / nesting 6; `runSubagent` is 209 / CC 70 / nesting 4; `compactNowImpl` is 63 / CC 17 / nesting 3; `driveOperation` is 47 / CC 17 / nesting 3. `make lint` now compares those named functions to that exact Git baseline, fails if a tracked function disappears or its cyclomatic complexity or control nesting increases, and reports line count without enforcing a global size cap. Extracted helpers require separate assessment. The focused tests passed 3/3; typecheck, Clippy, and fmt passed. PR #766's required Node 22/24, Linux, and test-shard checks passed at exact head `23a563ff31383136d7ad3b93ec59410f39bac631`; post-merge CI passed on `061843fc5fca4c874ac298093120b46073e50ab9` (`45319250946`).\n172-\n173-**Required tests / acceptance:** The focused tests prove that decision-count and nesting growth are rejected and that the tracked release-baseline functions pass. `make lint` runs in the required Node 22/24 and Linux CI gates. The gate also fails closed if a tracked function or baseline source cannot be resolved. The verification workflow remains changeable by the sole maintainer under the current single-user policy; independent protection is unavailable and must not be implied.\n174-\n175-**Migration / rollback:** If historical warnings appear on a new toolchain, baseline them narrowly with tracked expiry/owner; do not suppress globally. The sole maintainer may direct rollback of this specific gate; any rollback must be visible in the PR and must not be described as independently approved. Residual risk: static analysis cannot establish requirements correctness or provider behavior.\n176-\n177-## Dependency-aware order\n178-\n179:1. **WP-P10-02** aggregate budgets across direct, delegated, retry and resume paths (P0).\n180-2. **WP-P10-03** durable evidence and candidate/policy binding (P1; implement local integrity controls and state the single-maintainer trust limit).\n181:3. **WP-P10-04** aggregate resource admission and cleanup (P1; budget counters feed this work).\n182-4. **WP-P10-05** static-analysis/complexity gate (P2; reuse existing CI gates and state the single-maintainer trust limit).\n183-5. **WP-P10-01** remains deferred until a second independent maintainer is available; it is not a prerequisite for the work above or publication.\n184-\n185-Each implementable package lands independently with its own negative tests. CI green on a PR is not proof the implementation is correct; hosted checks must be tied to the exact candidate and reported alongside the single-maintainer review limitation.\n## codex/p10-02-file-side-effect-crash-recovery...origin/codex/p10-02-file-side-effect-crash-recovery\n00dc078fdc16268247baec0a02801b318b63d099\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":2,"nanos":429392127}},"started_at_ms":1795854496175,"completed_at_ms":1795854498606}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-28T08:28:19.456Z","ordinal":317827,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a22144-fe80-7a43-a432-c573a9fe87c8","call_id":"call_d0f45ae9b89f453ebfac44ebf85ddf4e","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 3.5 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 13929)\nTotal output lines: 166\n\n22-\n23-**Required tests / acceptance:** Record the configured ruleset and required status checks at the exact revision; report independent approval as unavailable. This package is not a prerequisite for implementation, PR publication, or release work under the current owner decision.\n24-\n25-**Migration / rollback:** No repository policy change is proposed. Residual risk: a sole maintainer can change acceptance code and its checks; exact-head hosted CI and candid self-review do not provide independent review.\n26-\n27:## WP-P10-02 — Durable task-level execution budgets\n28-\n29-**Priority:** P0 · **Complexity:** L · **Dependencies:** WP-P10-01 recommended first; can be implemented independently.\n30-\n31-**Gap/evidence:** Direct objective continuation has finite total-step, cumulative objective elapsed-time, and per-invocation time ceilings (`cli/src/types.ts`, `cli/src/core/run_turn.ts`, `cli/src/core/execution_tracking.ts`). Durable operations enforce aggregate active wall time across their driver interval and, when an optional token cap is configured, roll up child token usage and fail closed when measured usage is unavailable. PR #857 also puts provider retries, same-turn side requests, and in-process nested delegates under the persisted inference-submission counter for a durable objective. PR #869 adds a separate durable-operation counter shared across its work units. A single persisted accounting scope across every provider call path has not been demonstrated; calls made outside those objective or operation scopes and arbitrary side-effect outcomes after process or host failure still require investigation and reconciliation.\n32-\n33-**Affected surfaces:** `types.ts`, `config.ts`, `run_turn.ts`, subagent execution in `agent.ts`, provider recovery, tool deadlines, durable objective/task state, operation runner and CLI/UI reporting.\n34-\n35-**Design / reuse:** Extend `ContinuationBudget` and existing durable objective/operation records with a task budget id, maximum total steps/tool invocations/inference submissions/elapsed time/tokens, per-tool deadline and delegation depth. Persist consumed counters before/after side effects with `withDurableSync`. Child agents inherit the parent’s remaining budget; provider retry consumes the same inference allowance. A resumed task cannot regain budget. Require explicit operator-authorized budget renewal after exhaustion.\n36-\n37-**Implementation steps:**\n38-\n39-1. Define additive schema fields with migration defaults that do not silently claim prior counters are zero; old tasks resume in a conservative bounded mode or require explicit operator choice.\n40-2. Add a shared budget tracker passed through direct turns, transient in-turn subagents, durable work units, provider recovery and tool dispatch.\n41-3. Enforce before direct and delegated dispatch and before any retry; classify exhaustion as `BLOCKED`/`LIMIT_REACHED`, never `VERIFIED` or successful operation completion.\n42-4. Add ceilings for response streaming and whole-turn wall time, including a finite total timeout for local inference streams; idle timeout remains separate.\n43-5. Report dimension, configured limit, consumed amount and resume behavior in CLI/TUI result.\n44-\n45-**Required tests / acceptance:** Model emits distinct writes indefinitely: run stops at exact step/tool/token/time budget with nonzero/non-success result. Nested subagent shares remaining budget. Repeated provider failures consume retry allowance. Exhaustion followed by process restart and resume does not reset counters. Resume near limit permits only remaining budget. A hung local tool and slow-drip stream stop by configured deadline. Budget persistence write failure prevents new side-effectful dispatch.\n46-\n47-**Migration / rollback:** New config defaults may shorten formerly unlimited runs; provide an explicit operator override with a hard documented maximum, not Infinity. Keep old records readable. Rollback must not turn persisted exhausted state into zero; older binaries should refuse unsupported budget schema versions. Residual risk: already-running external calls may finish after cancellation or have unknown outcomes.\n48-\n49-**Progress (2026-11-22):** The `runTurnImpl` path persists a per-objective tool invocation ceiling (default 1,000; hard maximum 10,000), admits calls before dispatch, and checkpoints the count before dispatcher effects. In-turn `runSubagent` children reserve from the same tracker and persist each reservation before dispatch; exhaustion and failed persistence both stop the child before another effect. A saved ceiling cannot be raised by a later invocation. Legacy execution records explicitly mark tool history incomplete, so migrated counts are not presented as exact. Tests cover bounded direct and delegated calls, resume without budget renewal, admission bounds, legacy lower-bound tracking, failed delegated persistence, and workspace-observer classification. This tranche does not yet cover durable work units, provider recovery/retries, inference or token accounting, cumulative elapsed limits, per-tool deadlines, or process-crash reconciliation.\n50-\n51-**Progress (2026-11-23, merged PR #760):** The primary turn's provider-recovery loop reserves each request, including retries, against a persisted objective ceiling (default 1,000; hard maximum 10,000). Persistence failure prevents dispatch; resume can lower but not raise the saved ceiling; legacy records expose earlier inference history as unknown. The feature test and all required exact-head checks passed at merge commit `51bf3db79fdadca26dbde6bbf752c9982c7793e3`; post-merge CI passed on that merge commit. This is not yet a ceiling on all model requests: context-findings extraction and LLM compaction summary/requirement-probe calls do not reserve from it, nor do delegated `spawn_subagent` requests or durable operation work units. Without durable objective state, the counter comes from `ExecutionTracker.fresh()` and resets per turn. `NEXUS_MAX_INFERENCE_SUBMISSIONS` may be selected in a project `.env` up to 10,000; on resume, an existing saved ceiling can only be lowered. Availability probes are excluded. **P10-02 remains incomplete.** Remaining scope: include all side and delegated requests in a shared durable pre-dispatch counter; account for durable operation units, tokens and cumulative elapsed limits; add per-tool deadlines and process-crash reconciliation.\n52-\n53:**Follow-up progress (2026-11-26, merged PR #857):** Each `runTurn` installs an `AsyncLocalStorage` inference-admission scope. `client.ts` consults every active scope before each provider completion request, while the durable objective reserves and persists its counter before dispatch. Nested in-process turns inherit the enclosing scope, so provider retries/failovers, same-turn side requests, and nested delegates consume the same objective allowance; persistence failure prevents the provider call. `cli/test/p10_inference_submission_budget.test.ts` covers finite limits and overrides, retry accounting and resume, side requests and nested delegates, persisted parent allowance, failed persistence, and legacy unknown history. Required CI run `45739706503` and CodeQL run `45739706508` passed on merge commit `d5e3f602c721fdaf213d1031d1616364c126bf43` (PR #857). This does not create a shared counter across distinct durable operation units; their request counts remain separate. **WP-P10-02 remains incomplete** for operation-wide inference-request accounting and arbitrary side-effect recovery after process or host failure.\n54-\n55-**Progress (2026-11-24, merged PR #762):** `runTurnImpl` arms a timer for the active turn's `maxTurnMinutes` deadline and requests cancellation while inference or a tool is still awaiting completion. Expiry returns `LIMIT_REACHED` / `TIME_BUDGET_EXHAUSTED`; the timer is cleared on normal completion and caught failure. Tests cover aborting slow streamed inference, stopping a running shell command before its later side effect, and c…9929 tokens truncated…chiveCapacity` scans archive files under the existing state-store guard, rejects unreadable/non-regular entries and scans above 100,000 entries, and refuses a write before archive append or fork creation if it would cross the cap. It preserves existing archives and does not add another cleanup or admission framework. The new `session_archive_bounds.test.ts` covers aggregate accounting across sessions, byte preservation on refusal, fork refusal before state creation, inability to raise the ceiling through options, and empty append behavior at a full quota. With the worktree-local Rust core built, `session_archive_bounds.test.ts`, `session_fork.test.ts`, and `compaction_continuity.test.ts` passed **43/43** on Node 24.11.1 (exit 0); process-death recovery cases and the existing archive-write failure case were included. After the final empty-append guard, the current archive and fork suites passed **10/10**, `npm --prefix cli run build` passed, and `git diff --check` passed. PR #919 merged at `2026-11-27T20:00:55Z` as `a944a4eac448fdd1b08f1adfdca5be5696376ad2`. Required PR-head CI run `46059219684` and CodeQL run `46059219707` passed at exact PR head `d1778581428312574f24057e5b2741e6d3aae802`. Post-merge CI run `46060674463` passed both Node test gates, all eight shards, Linux packaging, and diff coverage; CodeQL run `46060674335` also passed on the exact merge commit. Entire Gates failed on the PR head but is advisory and excluded from required merge gates by project policy. Scope is limited to `*.history.jsonl` archives written by participating current-version Nexus processes; this is not a general disk quota and does not cap other state, external writers, or older running versions. **WP-P10-04 remains partially open** for the broader CPU/memory, session-count, provider-accounting, and cleanup/recovery gaps above.\n156-\n157:**Progress (2026-11-28, merged PR #934):** The normal CLI and sidecar lifecycle reserve a named slot through the existing `SharedResourceAdmission` before using state. Admission is scoped per canonical home and defaults to 64 concurrent client processes; the launching environment may lower `NEXUS_MAX_ACTIVE_CLIENTS` from 1 to 64 because admission runs before project `.env` loading. The kernel releases slots on process exit, and the existing exclusive restore lease remains unchanged. A 16-process same-home regression reproduced false capacity errors under zero-wait guard contention, then passed in three consecutive runs with the bounded 10-second guard wait; genuine slot exhaustion still fails closed. `npm run build` passed on Node 24.11.1. Required exact-head checks passed at PR head `4f55c6d4bfd4a58f480816b5871d5b97c36a0221`: both Node test matrices, all eight test shards, Linux build, JavaScript/TypeScript, and CodeQL. PR #934 merged at `2026-11-28T07:16:02Z` as `9b415dfdf8b754dd67a6cbb7b0a5084a1bfe9395`; post-merge CI run `46120407959` and CodeQL run `46120408066` passed on that exact merge commit. Entire Gates failed on the PR head, but is advisory and not a required merge check. This bounds cooperating Nexus CLI processes per canonical home on one Linux host, not in-process sessions, aggregate CPU/memory, separate homes, or non-Nexus processes. **WP-P10-04 remains partially open** for the other aggregate resource, provider-accounting, and cleanup/recovery gaps above.\n158-\n159-## WP-P10-05 — Compiler/static-analysis gate and hotspot metrics\n160-\n161-**Priority:** P2 · **Complexity:** S/M · **Dependencies:** none; independent protection of CI remains unavailable under WP-P10-01.\n162-\n163-**Gap/evidence:** Strict TS build, CodeQL JS/TS, Clippy, and diff-scoped rustfmt are present in CI. No Rust CodeQL or targeted complexity gate was found. `runTurnImpl` remains a critical large function.\n164-\n165-**Affected surfaces:** `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, `core/Cargo.toml`, TS configuration and targeted complexity documentation.\n166-\n167-**Design / reuse:** Reuse the existing `make lint` gate for typecheck, Clippy, and changed-file rustfmt; do not add a duplicate CI job. Keep Rust warnings scoped and justified rather than inventing a large analyzer bundle. Add complexity reporting or thresholds only for critical high-change functions after measuring the baseline; avoid arbitrary whole-repository size caps.\n168-\n169-**Progress (2026-11-23, `main` at `9156571f7d5fd8f8436c3105f0237628ab35e857`):** The Clippy CI gate proposed above is already implemented through `make lint` (`Makefile:24-27`), which runs CLI typecheck, Clippy with `-D warnings`, and diff-scoped rustfmt. CI calls this target in `test-matrix (22)`, `test-matrix (24)`, and `linux-x86_64` (`.github/workflows/ci.yml:74-75,196-197`). Workflow run `45047617708` completed successfully on that exact main SHA, including all three static-gate steps. Do not add a duplicate Clippy job. Remaining work in this package is a narrowly scoped, evidence-based complexity metric and its enforcement policy; the independent protection dependency from WP-P10-01 remains unavailable in this single-user repository.\n170-\n171-**Progress (2026-11-24, merged PR #766):** Measured the critical-path baseline at release commit `4d9996d485c2db866918c110d35e1c1a1d3b2911` using Babel's TypeScript parser. `runTurnImpl` is 1,262 lines / CC 324 / nesting 5; `dispatchToolCallImpl` is 900 / CC 288 / nesting 6; `runSubagent` is 209 / CC 70 / nesting 4; `compactNowImpl` is 63 / CC 17 / nesting 3; `driveOperation` is 47 / CC 17 / nesting 3. `make lint` now compares those named functions to that exact Git baseline, fails if a tracked function disappears or its cyclomatic complexity or control nesting increases, and reports line count without enforcing a global size cap. Extracted helpers require separate assessment. The focused tests passed 3/3; typecheck, Clippy, and fmt passed. PR #766's required Node 22/24, Linux, and test-shard checks passed at exact head `23a563ff31383136d7ad3b93ec59410f39bac631`; post-merge CI passed on `061843fc5fca4c874ac298093120b46073e50ab9` (`45319250946`).\n172-\n173-**Required tests / acceptance:** The focused tests prove that decision-count and nesting growth are rejected and that the tracked release-baseline functions pass. `make lint` runs in the required Node 22/24 and Linux CI gates. The gate also fails closed if a tracked function or baseline source cannot be resolved. The verification workflow remains changeable by the sole maintainer under the current single-user policy; independent protection is unavailable and must not be implied.\n174-\n175-**Migration / rollback:** If historical warnings appear on a new toolchain, baseline them narrowly with tracked expiry/owner; do not suppress globally. The sole maintainer may direct rollback of this specific gate; any rollback must be visible in the PR and must not be described as independently approved. Residual risk: static analysis cannot establish requirements correctness or provider behavior.\n176-\n177-## Dependency-aware order\n178-\n179:1. **WP-P10-02** aggregate budgets across direct, delegated, retry and resume paths (P0).\n180-2. **WP-P10-03** durable evidence and candidate/policy binding (P1; implement local integrity controls and state the single-maintainer trust limit).\n181:3. **WP-P10-04** aggregate resource admission and cleanup (P1; budget counters feed this work).\n182-4. **WP-P10-05** static-analysis/complexity gate (P2; reuse existing CI gates and state the single-maintainer trust limit).\n183-5. **WP-P10-01** remains deferred until a second independent maintainer is available; it is not a prerequisite for the work above or publication.\n184-\n185-Each implementable package lands independently with its own negative tests. CI green on a PR is not proof the implementation is correct; hosted checks must be tied to the exact candidate and reported alongside the single-maintainer review limitation.\n## codex/p10-02-file-side-effect-crash-recovery...origin/codex/p10-02-file-side-effect-crash-recovery\n00dc078fdc16268247baec0a02801b318b63d099\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a22143-fcf9-7462-927e-e66d27ee2285","create_time":1795854499.4565642}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":139977,"sampled_through_sequence":139974,"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
