{"timestamp":"2026-11-28T08:28:23.006Z","ordinal":317832,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","turn_id":"01a22143-fcf9-7462-927e-e66d27ee2285","session_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","root_turn_id":"01a22143-fcf9-7462-927e-e66d27ee2285","response_id":"resp_033f353a673dbf89016b0a90a3b9ac87d2835caeda508c540d","usage":{"input_tokens":167463,"cached_input_tokens":163072,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":144,"reasoning_output_tokens":59,"total_tokens":167607},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":818880,"cached_input_tokens":810880,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":1146,"reasoning_output_tokens":550,"total_tokens":820026},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":6606890044,"cached_input_tokens":6532636672,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":15246993,"reasoning_output_tokens":8979103,"total_tokens":6622137037}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-28T08:28:24.280Z","ordinal":317833,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","turn_id":"01a22143-fcf9-7462-927e-e66d27ee2285","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-8c0af72f-b3db-4f8e-b41b-5657ac0ee707","process_id":"55889","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '51,100p' docs/assurance/POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-wt/p10-02-file-side-effect-crash-recovery","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '51,100p' docs/assurance/POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md","name":"POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md","path":"docs/assurance/POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"**Progress (2026-11-23, merged PR #760):** The primary turn's provider-recovery loop reserves each request, including retries, against a persisted objective ceiling (default 1,000; hard maximum 10,000). Persistence failure prevents dispatch; resume can lower but not raise the saved ceiling; legacy records expose earlier inference history as unknown. The feature test and all required exact-head checks passed at merge commit `51bf3db79fdadca26dbde6bbf752c9982c7793e3`; post-merge CI passed on that merge commit. This is not yet a ceiling on all model requests: context-findings extraction and LLM compaction summary/requirement-probe calls do not reserve from it, nor do delegated `spawn_subagent` requests or durable operation work units. Without durable objective state, the counter comes from `ExecutionTracker.fresh()` and resets per turn. `NEXUS_MAX_INFERENCE_SUBMISSIONS` may be selected in a project `.env` up to 10,000; on resume, an existing saved ceiling can only be lowered. Availability probes are excluded. **P10-02 remains incomplete.** Remaining scope: include all side and delegated requests in a shared durable pre-dispatch counter; account for durable operation units, tokens and cumulative elapsed limits; add per-tool deadlines and process-crash reconciliation.\n\n**Follow-up progress (2026-11-26, merged PR #857):** Each `runTurn` installs an `AsyncLocalStorage` inference-admission scope. `client.ts` consults every active scope before each provider completion request, while the durable objective reserves and persists its counter before dispatch. Nested in-process turns inherit the enclosing scope, so provider retries/failovers, same-turn side requests, and nested delegates consume the same objective allowance; persistence failure prevents the provider call. `cli/test/p10_inference_submission_budget.test.ts` covers finite limits and overrides, retry accounting and resume, side requests and nested delegates, persisted parent allowance, failed persistence, and legacy unknown history. Required CI run `45739706503` and CodeQL run `45739706508` passed on merge commit `d5e3f602c721fdaf213d1031d1616364c126bf43` (PR #857). This does not create a shared counter across distinct durable operation units; their request counts remain separate. **WP-P10-02 remains incomplete** for operation-wide inference-request accounting and arbitrary side-effect recovery after process or host failure.\n\n**Progress (2026-11-24, merged PR #762):** `runTurnImpl` arms a timer for the active turn's `maxTurnMinutes` deadline and requests cancellation while inference or a tool is still awaiting completion. Expiry returns `LIMIT_REACHED` / `TIME_BUDGET_EXHAUSTED`; the timer is cleared on normal completion and caught failure. Tests cover aborting slow streamed inference, stopping a running shell command before its later side effect, and clearing the timer after a normal turn. Exact-head CI passed on PR #762 (`45293148069`); post-merge CI passed at `6b916195d57ddd4b641214ea3e835f10eb722a28` (`45294542653`). This bounded one active direct turn; PR #774 later added cumulative elapsed accounting across resumes of that durable objective. A separate per-tool deadline is still absent. **P10-02 remains incomplete.** Remaining scope includes shared durable accounting for side/delegated model requests and durable operation units, operation-wide elapsed limits, per-tool deadlines, and process-crash reconciliation.\n\n**Progress (2026-11-24, merged PR #774):** The direct durable-objective elapsed-time ceiling is configurable with `--max-task-minutes` / `NEXUS_MAX_TASK_MINUTES` (default and hard maximum 4,320 minutes). Active intervals are checkpointed before inference or tools; resume charges an unclosed interval, cannot raise its saved ceiling, and legacy records conservatively estimate from `objective.startedAt` or fail closed when that timestamp is unavailable. A backward wall-clock change exhausts the remaining budget rather than restoring it. The additive elapsed fields are included in the `run --json` contract without changing its schema version. PR #774's required checks passed at head `02e1d7550ea48bb91cbf5ba0c67a69deb88d4baf`; it merged as `45520edcf33569802698f58c43277cad1e04a0a4`. Post-merge CI run `45374543474` and CodeQL run `45374543479` completed successfully on that merge commit, which is an ancestor of the current release baseline `257d324d61bbd1957a464f5ca6312d49d18e1c68`. This ceiling applies to one durable objective ledger; it does not cap aggregate elapsed work across all units in a durable operation. **WP-P10-02 remains incomplete:** shared accounting for side/delegated requests, operation-wide elapsed limits, per-tool deadlines, and process-crash/unknown-outcome reconciliation remain.\n\n**Progress (2026-11-25, implementation in progress):** The durable operation runner now has a finite aggregate active wall-time cap (default and hard maximum 4,320 minutes) shared across concurrently executing units. Each active interval is recorded in the operation record; an open interval is charged on recovery, and migrated records conservatively use record age because earlier active time cannot be reconstructed. Expiry aborts the current operation signal, pauses the operation, and records interrupted side effects as unknown so resume cannot silently replay them. The existing `nexus operation budget` command now audits token and active-time cap changes together, and operation status/JSON reports elapsed, remaining, and uncertain legacy history. The implementation deliberately measures the operation driver's wall interval, not the sum of worker CPU time or time spent parked between runs. Focused evidence on the current branch: `p10_operation_active_budget.test.ts` (5/5), `goal_pa3_completion.test.ts` (8/8), `operation_runner_refactor.test.ts` (3/3), `operation_schema.test.ts` (19/19), `operation_view.test.ts` (35/35), and `npm run build` passed. This remains one tranche, not complete WP-P10-02: side/delegated inference requests, token accounting across all operation work, per-tool deadlines, process crash during arbitrary side effects, and durable unknown-outcome reconciliation beyond the in-flight budget-expiry boundary remain open. Exact-head hosted checks and post-merge CI have not yet run for this change.\n\n**Progress (2026-11-26, RPC worker timeout tranche, merged PR #862):** Generic Rust RPC deadlines and cancellation now retire the worker process rather than only ending the bridge wait. A retiring worker accepts no new dispatch; sibling in-flight calls resolve as outcome unknown; stdin EOF requests the Rust worker's normal cleanup; and a bounded grace period ends a worker that does not exit. Command cancellation retains its runner confirmation window before retirement. Focused evidence: `rpc_worker_timeout.test.ts` covers unanswered RPC, sibling unknown outcomes, cancellation, bounded forced exit, and a blocked real Rust filesystem RPC followed by clean worker replacement; `worker_stdin_epipe.test.ts` guards existing disconnect handling; `npm --prefix cli run build` passed. Required PR checks and CodeQL passed at exact head `8ea727e878efea7750e811c8836542299e704e30`; post-merge CI run `45760041918` and CodeQL run `45760041919` passed on release merge `1c48520411d3ddff2dbe8f2bb33cc922394e5e7d`. This closes the unowned in-flight worker portion of per-tool deadlines, but does not add configurable per-tool deadlines, guarantee cancellation of kernel-blocked I/O, or resolve arbitrary side-effect outcomes after process termination.\n\n**Progress (2026-11-26, tool timeout hard bound):** `NEXUS_MAX_TOOL_TIMEOUT_SECS` is now parsed as a strict positive integer and capped at 86,400 seconds. This keeps the command runner's outer RPC timer within Node's supported timer range. Before the cap, `NEXUS_MAX_TOOL_TIMEOUT_SECS=2147484` produced a `2,147,489,000ms` bridge timer; a direct Node probe showed that an oversized timer fires after 1ms, which could prematurely retire the worker and return an unknown result. The regression test verifies oversized and malformed operator values and that the bridge timeout remains below `2,147,483,647ms`. This initially bounded the command timeout path; the non-command bridge input path is addressed by the follow-up below. WP-P10-02 remains incomplete for aggregate inference-request accounting across durable work units and arbitrary side-effect recovery.\n\n**Follow-up progress (2026-11-26, non-command RPC timeout input bound):** `rpcDeadlines` now accepts a non-command `timeout_secs` only when it is a positive safe integer, caps it using the existing `maxToolTimeoutSecs()` operator limit, and otherwise uses the finite 35-second RPC default. This also prevents untrusted bridge inputs from overflowing Node timers. The regression was reproduced before the fix: `Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER` yielded a `9,007,199,254,740,996,000ms` bridge delay. The test covers the hard cap, a lower operator override, negative/fractional/non-finite/unsafe inputs, and default fallback. Local evidence on Node 22.23.5 and 24.11.1: focused timeout, process-bound and RPC-worker suites passed 15/15 on each runtime; `make build`, `make lint`, and `git diff --check` passed. This closes timeout arithmetic for the covered bridge paths, but does not resolve aggregate inference-request accounting across durable work units or arbitrary side-effect recovery; WP-P10-02 remains incomplete.\n\n**Follow-up progress (2026-11-26, merged PR #869):** Durable operation state now stores a finite `maxInferenceCalls` policy (default 1,000; hard maximum 10,000), the consumed `inferenceCalls` count, and whether historical usage is known. Each provider submission made under an operation work-unit execution scope reserves and persists one count before dispatch; persistence failure prevents the request. Older records migrate with unknown history and pause before dispatch until the operator explicitly starts an accounting epoch. Reaching the cap pauses the operation, and changing the cap preserves the consumed count. The existing `nexus operation budget` command configures or explicitly resets this accounting and reports usage. The five tests in `p10_operation_inference_budget.test.ts` cover limits, cross-unit counting and exhaustion, cap changes and legacy reset, unknown legacy history, and failed reservation persistence. Local evidence: the CLI build passed and the focused operation origin, semantic conflict, and team convergence suites passed 62/62. Required Node 22/24 matrix, Linux, and CodeQL checks passed on the exact PR merge ref `ae1b0d560153985b2c94a9d095ce5ecbcab11d51`; PR #869 merged as `50207a6781ee1152950f0d42a3087aee034cc790`. Post-merge CI run `45807999265` and CodeQL run `45807999264` both passed on that release merge. This closes aggregate inference submission accounting across durable operation work units, but does not establish one counter across every possible provider call path or resolve arbitrary side-effect outcomes after process or host failure. **WP-P10-02 remains incomplete.**\n\n**Follow-up progress (2026-11-26, merged PR #877):** `collectOperationUsage` provides the shared read-only rollup of child-task token records for operation admission, goal accounting, operation status and budget views. It separates reported from estimated tokens and marks missing, malformed, and in-progress usage incomplete instead of presenting it as exact. Operation view schema v4 adds `tokenUsage`. PR #877 merged as `443f52b6871fedf4d65f0a99d10b5c41cdc91d7c`; post-merge CI run `45877783322` and CodeQL run `45877783393` passed on that commit. This rollup makes accounting and reporting consistent but is not a hard per-request token ceiling.\n\n**Follow-up progress (2026-11-26, merged PR #881):** Before an operation can become terminal `COMPLETE`, `OperationRunner` rechecks the persisted token rollup when `maxTotalTokens` is configured. It pauses on a measured overrun or incomplete/unavailable usage, while allowing exact-cap completion when no further work needs dispatch; the completed unit remains available for operator review. The focused token-reporting, inference-budget, and goal-lifecycle tests passed 21/21. PR #881 merged as `5061a3e83ce6daf34f34785bd3753f11604ba77d`; post-merge CI run `45896185332` and CodeQL run `45896185330` passed. This prevents false terminal success after the last batch but does not stop a dispatched request or concurrent batch from exceeding the configured token cap.\n\n**Follow-up progress (2026-11-26, merged PR #883):** Operation schema v10 persists cumulative request-token estimates. The existing async-local admission hook carries an estimate based on serialized input length plus the configured output allowance; operation scope reserves it with the inference submission count before dispatch. Concurrent calls cannot reserve the same remaining estimate, reservations persist across resume and are not refunded after cancellation or unknown outcomes, and reservation failure prevents dispatch. The final completion gate still checks persisted child usage. Generated ACP, run-result, and sidecar schemas/ledgers were updated. Focused reservation, migration, semantic-conflict tests passed 17/17, and protocol contract tests passed 30/30. PR #883 merged as `09f28d7e73633716859d1c1bb1edd30f26b5a7c6`; post-merge CI run `45908696882` and CodeQL run `45908696908` passed.\n\n**Follow-up progress (2026-11-27, merged PR #885):** The request admission scope now narrows the provider output allowance to the remaining operation estimate and serializes the limit for OpenAI Chat, OpenAI Responses, Anthropic, Gemini, and Bedrock. If a provider rejects the required limit, or the active protocol cannot represent it, the request is not retried without the cap. The prompt-side token estimate remains heuristic, and Nexus cannot prove that each remote provider honors a serialized limit; this is not an exact or hard provider-spend ceiling. The focused provider/operation set passed 160/160, with build and lint passing. PR #885 merged as `d8d2fe2ab8698286660db8d9d78d4f2d5aedc74a`; post-merge CI run `45917052347` and CodeQL run `45917052388` passed.\n\n**Process-crash recovery evidence (2026-11-27, merged PR #887):** A separate Nexus process using a local fake gateway is killed after `run_command` has written a side-effect marker but before its result is saved. Resume reports the interrupted effect as unknown and refuses to replay it; the marker remains at one. The test passed on Node 22 and 24. PR #887 merged as `9bfe2ed86bfcf092e0406e71b83cb5b3e7e2ce62`; post-merge CI run `45927066566` and CodeQL run `45927066332` passed.\n\n**Follow-up process-crash sibling matrix (PR #894):** The real-process regression also covers an ordered two-call response: the first `run_command` completes and is persisted; a second writes an effect and remains in flight when Nexus is killed. Resume retains the first result, reports the second as unknown, and does not repeat either effect. The focused test passed 2/2. PR #894 merged as `5509e1f59ccced4f4e470cdd2ba5006bd73dcd77`; post-merge CI run `45947491306` and CodeQL run `45947491736` passed.\n\n**Provider-request crash accounting (PR #895):** With a local fake gateway holding a provider response after receipt, the test kills Nexus and resumes with a higher configured limit; the saved request count remains 1/1 and Nexus sends no second request. The focused test passed on Node 22 and 24. PR #895 merged as `91e9633cbaee9f6a9dca00cacf94f50e25c1b97c`; post-merge CI run `45951101344` and CodeQL run `45951101366` passed.\n\n**Provider-outcome recovery (PR #901):** Each provider request in objective and durable-operation scopes receives a persisted reservation before dispatch, then settles that specific reservation when Nexus observes a response. On resume, leftover in-flight requests are recorded as unknown before further work; legacy migrations do not invent prior outcomes. This replaces the earlier provider-crash test's weaker “budget exhausted, so no retry” evidence with an explicit unknown-outcome record. The request stays charged; this does not establish provider completion, billing, or exactly-once execution. Required checks passed at PR head `d0053ae4f7b78a3c23d26caf376665184f4020c9`; PR #901 merged as `af10e7b93c2bf2142cd738bda38aa575698c5813`, with post-merge CI run `46001827052` and CodeQL run `46001826978` passing on that commit.\n\n**Durable mutation-intent failure gate (PR #924, merged as `d4feb4d925d22f8d5517d306f5b13df8e8eec2a9`):** A fault-injected failure of `recordOperationStart` previously still allowed `write_file` to execute and create its target. Durable mutating tool calls now return a failed, not-executed receipt when the start record cannot be persisted (including when the durable state manager is unavailable); they do not capture a baseline or dispatch the handler, and they do not append an unmatched end record. If the current task record has not yet been materialized, the existing durable checkpoint path writes it before the operation start; a checkpoint failure also blocks dispatch. The `p10_operation_journal_fail_closed.test.ts` regression exercises a real fake-provider request and Rust file-write handler with start-journal persistence forced to fail. This closes the observed start-record gap for durable registered mutating tools. It does not cover transient non-durable subagent calls, prove host/power-loss behavior, or change the existing degraded-recovery treatment when an end-record write fails. Local verification included the focused regression, `npm run build`, `make lint`, and `git diff --check`. The first PR commit failed the hosted critical-complexity gate because `executeToolCall` increased from CC 43 to 51; helpers reduced it to CC 30. The first exact-head hosted run exposed test fixtures that replaced the constructor's durable store without materializing the task record; checkpoint-before-journal now handles those supported lazy-checkpoint entry paths. All exact-head required CI checks passed on `a06a00d01895b11aaec4de2227b8eff5e3586ec4` (CI run `46089209876`, CodeQL run `46089209866`); advisory Entire Gates failed and did not block the merge under repository policy. Post-merge CI run `46090864177` and CodeQL run `46090864175` passed on `d4feb4d925d22f8d5517d306f5b13df8e8eec2a9`.\n\n**Per-tool Rust RPC deadline slice (PR #922):** `NEXUS_TOOL_TIMEOUT_SECS_<TOOL>` sets a Rust RPC method's default timeout and ceiling; request-level values can shorten it, while `NEXUS_MAX_TOOL_TIMEOUT_SECS` remains the global hard cap. `run_command` receives the effective value in its runner request and keeps the longer bridge grace; a timed-out non-command RPC retires its shared worker and reports pending outcomes as unknown. Local `make build`, `make lint`, and `git diff --check` passed. On Node 22.22.2 and 24.11.1, `verifier_timeout.test.ts` passed 8/8, `rpc_worker_timeout.test.ts` 6/6, `process_bounds_and_services.test.ts` 3/3, and `tool_surface_characterization.test.ts` 6/6. PR #922 merged as `ed55744f8fda33dcc6410a664e9e50bd62d7d6f3`; required checks passed on the exact merge candidate `83a17fe8f2c3dce3e102b572c7ab8e18d144dce5`, and post-merge CI run `46078951080` plus CodeQL run `46078951108` passed on the release merge commit.\n\n**Provider request deadline check (release head `a1f07419ba63402eb5f01dfe85783ae282aa9817`):** `LocalLlmClient.createWatchdog` applies a finite whole-request deadline to inference requests (900,000 ms default; 3,600,000 ms hard maximum) in addition to first-byte, idle, and caller-cancellation signals (`cli/src/client.ts:361-369,1154-1175`). On Node 24.11.1, `provider_robustness.test.ts` and `inference_transport.test.ts` passed 46/46, including an always-trickling stream stopped by the total deadline, a silent-server timeout, and operator cancellation. This demonstrates a local client request deadline for the exercised inference paths. It does not prove a remote provider stops processing or billing after local cancellation, and it is not an exact provider-spend ceiling.\n\n**Plugin execution boundary (closed by PR #932):** The pre-fix findings above showed that asynchronous TypeScript plugin hooks could outlive their caller and that late middleware continuation could dispatch a tool after timeout. PR #932 (`3394b3a429d806256ab07936d62ed0586eaec907`, merged as `d0276535118aa197fc56371ab697220e20d5fd43`) moves file-loaded plugin callbacks into persistent, killable worker processes using versioned JSON IPC, a 1 MiB message cap, a 64 KiB combined stdout/stderr cap, and finite per-callback deadlines (default 15 seconds, maximum 60 seconds). Middleware continuation remains parent-owned; malformed or late messages fail closed; possibly-started custom tools and continuations report unknown outcomes; cancellation and shutdown retire workers. Workspace plugins run from a private temporary copy of the exact approved bytes. Local `make build`, `npm run build`, and `npm run typecheck` passed; the 82-test plugin/trust/policy compatibility set passed on Node 24.11.1, and focused containment tests passed 13/13 on both Node 22.22.2 and Node 24.11.1. Adversarial coverage includes synchronous infinite loops, never-settling callbacks, late continuation, timeout after continuation, cancellation, malformed/oversized IPC, and ordinary/detached child cleanup. Required PR checks passed at the exact head (Node 22/24 gates and matrices, all eight test shards, Linux packaging, and CodeQL); post-merge CI run `46107372969` and CodeQL run `46107372886` passed on merge commit `d0276535118aa197fc56371ab697220e20d5fd43`. This is execution containment, not an OS security sandbox or CPU/memory quota. Windows/macOS process-tree cleanup, host-crash cleanup, aggregate resource limits, and same-user deliberate protocol manipulation remain unproven.\n\n**WP-P10-02 status and remaining limits:** Merged changes provide operation-wide inference-submission reservations, estimated-token admission, provider-serialized output limits, a terminal measured-usage gate, persisted unknown provider outcomes after process interruption, process-crash evidence for selected command/provider paths, and bounded plugin callback execution from PR #932. PR #922 adds per-tool deadline defaults/caps for Rust RPC calls. Local provider inference requests also have a finite whole-request client deadline, with focused Node 24 tests passing 46/46 as recorded above; remote provider processing/billing after local abort remains unknown. These controls do not demonstrate exact token usage or provider spend: input-token admission is heuristic and remote providers may ignore a serialized output cap. Plugin process containment does not impose aggregate CPU/memory quotas or establish host-crash cleanup; crash tests do not establish host/power-loss safety or unknown-outcome recovery for every side-effectful tool/provider path. **WP-P10-02 remains incomplete** for exact/provider-enforced cumulative token containment and recovery qualification for other side-effect classes and host/power failure.\n\n## WP-P10-03 — Acceptance evidence integrity and export\n\n**Priority:** P1 · **Complexity:** M · **Dependencies:** none for local evidence integrity; independent policy identity remains unavailable under WP-P10-01.\n\n**Existing capability:** `FlightRecorder` hash-chains persisted events and `FlightPlayer` reports altered, reordered, removed or unchained records (`cli/src/core/hash_chain.ts`, `cli/src/flight.ts`). GV-2 already exports bounded audit bundles with per-file SHA-256, a canonical manifest, hash-linked file entries, offline verification, and optional operator-key Ed25519 signatures (`cli/src/core/audit_bundle.ts`, `cli/src/audit_contract.ts`). `CandidateTracker` invalidates receipts on known mutations and recomputes the candidate identity before merge (`cli/src/core/candidate.ts`). These controls must be reused; this package does not need a second chain or evidence store.\n\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":961172310}},"started_at_ms":1795854503318,"completed_at_ms":1795854504280}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-28T08:28:24.538Z","ordinal":317834,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a22145-1259-7ba1-af2c-3d98b2919707","call_id":"call_143e4f37a797404e8bcceaaefca75190","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.5 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 5982)\nTotal output lines: 50\n\n**Progress (2026-11-23, merged PR #760):** The primary turn's provider-recovery loop reserves each request, including retries, against a persisted objective ceiling (default 1,000; hard maximum 10,000). Persistence failure prevents dispatch; resume can lower but not raise the saved ceiling; legacy records expose earlier inference history as unknown. The feature test and all required exact-head checks passed at merge commit `51bf3db79fdadca26dbde6bbf752c9982c7793e3`; post-merge CI passed on that merge commit. This is not yet a ceiling on all model requests: context-findings extraction and LLM compaction summary/requirement-probe calls do not reserve from it, nor do delegated `spawn_subagent` requests or durable operation work units. Without durable objective state, the counter comes from `ExecutionTracker.fresh()` and resets per turn. `NEXUS_MAX_INFERENCE_SUBMISSIONS` may be selected in a project `.env` up to 10,000; on resume, an existing saved ceiling can only be lowered. Availability probes are excluded. **P10-02 remains incomplete.** Remaining scope: include all side and delegated requests in a shared durable pre-dispatch counter; account for durable operation units, tokens and cumulative elapsed limits; add per-tool deadlines and process-crash reconciliation.\n\n**Follow-up progress (2026-11-26, merged PR #857):** Each `runTurn` installs an `AsyncLocalStorage` inference-admission scope. `client.ts` consults every active scope before each provider completion request, while the durable objective reserves and persists its counter before dispatch. Nested in-process turns inherit the enclosing scope, so provider retries/failovers, same-turn side requests, and nested delegates consume the same objective allowance; persistence failure prevents the provider call. `cli/test/p10_inference_submission_budget.test.ts` covers finite limits and overrides, retry accounting and resume, side requests and nested delegates, persisted parent allowance, failed persistence, and legacy unknown history. Required CI run `45739706503` and CodeQL run `45739706508` passed on merge commit `d5e3f602c721fdaf213d1031d1616364c126bf43` (PR #857). This does not create a shared counter across distinct durable operation units; their request counts remain separate. **WP-P10-02 remains incomplete** for operation-wide inference-request accounting and arbitrary side-effect recovery after process or host failure.\n\n**Progress (2026-11-24, merged PR #762):** `runTurnImpl` arms a timer for the active turn's `maxTurnMinutes` deadline and requests cancellation while inference or a tool is still awaiting completion. Expiry returns `LIMIT_REACHED` / `TIME_BUDGET_EXHAUSTED`; the timer is cleared on normal completion and caught failure. Tests cover aborting slow streamed inference, stopping a running shell command before its later side effect, and clearing the timer after a normal turn. Exact-head CI passed on PR #762 (`45293148069`); post-merge CI passed at `6b916195d57ddd4b641214ea3e835f10eb722a28` (`45294542653`). This bounded one active direct turn; PR #774 later added cumulative elapsed accounting across resumes of that durable objective. A separate per-tool deadline is still absent. **P10-02 remains incomplete.** Remaining scope includes shared durable accounting for side/delegated model requests and durable operation units, operation-wide elapsed limits, per-tool deadlines, and process-crash reconciliation.\n\n**Progress (2026-11-24, merged PR #774):** The direct durable-objective elapsed-time ceiling is configurable with `--max-task-minutes` / `NEXUS_MAX_TASK_MINUTES` (default and hard maximum 4,320 minutes). Active intervals are checkpointed before inference or tools; resume charges an unclosed interval, cannot raise its saved ceiling, and legacy records conservatively estimate from `objective.startedAt` or fail closed when that timestamp is unavailable. A backward wall-clock change exhausts the remaining budget rather than restoring it. The additive elapsed fields are included in the `run --json` contract without changing its schema version. PR #774's required checks passed at head `02e1d7550ea48bb91cbf5ba0c67a69deb88d4baf`; it merged as `45520edcf33569802698f58c43277cad1e04a0a4`. Post-merge CI run `45374543474` and CodeQL run `45374543479` completed successfully on that merge commit, which is an ancestor of the current release baseline `257d324d61bbd1957a464f5ca6312d49d18e1c68`. This ceiling applies to one durable objective ledger; it does not cap aggregate elapsed work across all units in a durable operation. **WP-P10-02 remains incomplete:** shared accounting for side/delegated requests, operation-wide elapsed limits, per-tool deadlines, and process-crash/unknown-outcome reconciliation remain.\n\n**Progress (2026-11-25, implementation in progress):** The durable operation runner now has a finite aggregate active wall-time cap (default and hard maximum 4,320 minutes) shared across concurrently executing units. Each active interval is recorded in the operation record; an open interval is charged on recovery, and migrated records conservatively use record age because earlier active time cannot be reconstructed. Expiry aborts the current operation signal, pauses the operation, and records interrupted side effects as unknown so resume cannot silently replay them. The existing `nexus operation budget` command now audits token and active-time cap changes together, and operation status/JSON reports elapsed, remaining, and uncertain legacy history. The implementation deliberately measures the operation driver's wall interval, not the sum of worker CPU time or time spent parked between runs. Focused evidence on the current branch: `p10_operation_active_budget.test.ts` (5/5), `goal_pa3_completion.test.ts` (8/8), `operation_runner_refactor.test.ts` (3/3), `operation_schema.test.ts` (19/19), `operation_view.test.ts` (35/35), and `npm run build` passed. This remains one tranche, not complete WP-P10-02: side/delegated inference requests, token accounting across all operation work, per-tool deadlines, process crash during arbitrary side effects, and durable unknown-outcome reconciliation beyond the in-flight budget-expiry boundary remain open. Exact-head hosted checks and post-merge CI have not yet run for this change.\n\n**Progress (2026-11-26, RPC worker timeout tranche, merged PR #862):** Generic Rust RPC deadlines and cancellation now retire the worker process rather than only ending the bridge wait. A retiring worker accepts no new dispatch; sibling in-flight calls resolve as outcome unknown; stdin EOF requests the Rust worker's normal cleanup; and a bounded grace period ends a worker that does not exit. Command cancellation retains its runner confirmation window before retirement. Focused evidence: `rpc_worker_timeout.test.ts` covers unanswered RPC, sibling unknown outcomes, cancellation, bounded forced exit, and a blocked real Rust filesystem RPC followed by clean worker replacement; `worker_stdin_epipe.test.ts` guards…2482 tokens truncated…een materialized, the existing durable checkpoint path writes it before the operation start; a checkpoint failure also blocks dispatch. The `p10_operation_journal_fail_closed.test.ts` regression exercises a real fake-provider request and Rust file-write handler with start-journal persistence forced to fail. This closes the observed start-record gap for durable registered mutating tools. It does not cover transient non-durable subagent calls, prove host/power-loss behavior, or change the existing degraded-recovery treatment when an end-record write fails. Local verification included the focused regression, `npm run build`, `make lint`, and `git diff --check`. The first PR commit failed the hosted critical-complexity gate because `executeToolCall` increased from CC 43 to 51; helpers reduced it to CC 30. The first exact-head hosted run exposed test fixtures that replaced the constructor's durable store without materializing the task record; checkpoint-before-journal now handles those supported lazy-checkpoint entry paths. All exact-head required CI checks passed on `a06a00d01895b11aaec4de2227b8eff5e3586ec4` (CI run `46089209876`, CodeQL run `46089209866`); advisory Entire Gates failed and did not block the merge under repository policy. Post-merge CI run `46090864177` and CodeQL run `46090864175` passed on `d4feb4d925d22f8d5517d306f5b13df8e8eec2a9`.\n\n**Per-tool Rust RPC deadline slice (PR #922):** `NEXUS_TOOL_TIMEOUT_SECS_<TOOL>` sets a Rust RPC method's default timeout and ceiling; request-level values can shorten it, while `NEXUS_MAX_TOOL_TIMEOUT_SECS` remains the global hard cap. `run_command` receives the effective value in its runner request and keeps the longer bridge grace; a timed-out non-command RPC retires its shared worker and reports pending outcomes as unknown. Local `make build`, `make lint`, and `git diff --check` passed. On Node 22.22.2 and 24.11.1, `verifier_timeout.test.ts` passed 8/8, `rpc_worker_timeout.test.ts` 6/6, `process_bounds_and_services.test.ts` 3/3, and `tool_surface_characterization.test.ts` 6/6. PR #922 merged as `ed55744f8fda33dcc6410a664e9e50bd62d7d6f3`; required checks passed on the exact merge candidate `83a17fe8f2c3dce3e102b572c7ab8e18d144dce5`, and post-merge CI run `46078951080` plus CodeQL run `46078951108` passed on the release merge commit.\n\n**Provider request deadline check (release head `a1f07419ba63402eb5f01dfe85783ae282aa9817`):** `LocalLlmClient.createWatchdog` applies a finite whole-request deadline to inference requests (900,000 ms default; 3,600,000 ms hard maximum) in addition to first-byte, idle, and caller-cancellation signals (`cli/src/client.ts:361-369,1154-1175`). On Node 24.11.1, `provider_robustness.test.ts` and `inference_transport.test.ts` passed 46/46, including an always-trickling stream stopped by the total deadline, a silent-server timeout, and operator cancellation. This demonstrates a local client request deadline for the exercised inference paths. It does not prove a remote provider stops processing or billing after local cancellation, and it is not an exact provider-spend ceiling.\n\n**Plugin execution boundary (closed by PR #932):** The pre-fix findings above showed that asynchronous TypeScript plugin hooks could outlive their caller and that late middleware continuation could dispatch a tool after timeout. PR #932 (`3394b3a429d806256ab07936d62ed0586eaec907`, merged as `d0276535118aa197fc56371ab697220e20d5fd43`) moves file-loaded plugin callbacks into persistent, killable worker processes using versioned JSON IPC, a 1 MiB message cap, a 64 KiB combined stdout/stderr cap, and finite per-callback deadlines (default 15 seconds, maximum 60 seconds). Middleware continuation remains parent-owned; malformed or late messages fail closed; possibly-started custom tools and continuations report unknown outcomes; cancellation and shutdown retire workers. Workspace plugins run from a private temporary copy of the exact approved bytes. Local `make build`, `npm run build`, and `npm run typecheck` passed; the 82-test plugin/trust/policy compatibility set passed on Node 24.11.1, and focused containment tests passed 13/13 on both Node 22.22.2 and Node 24.11.1. Adversarial coverage includes synchronous infinite loops, never-settling callbacks, late continuation, timeout after continuation, cancellation, malformed/oversized IPC, and ordinary/detached child cleanup. Required PR checks passed at the exact head (Node 22/24 gates and matrices, all eight test shards, Linux packaging, and CodeQL); post-merge CI run `46107372969` and CodeQL run `46107372886` passed on merge commit `d0276535118aa197fc56371ab697220e20d5fd43`. This is execution containment, not an OS security sandbox or CPU/memory quota. Windows/macOS process-tree cleanup, host-crash cleanup, aggregate resource limits, and same-user deliberate protocol manipulation remain unproven.\n\n**WP-P10-02 status and remaining limits:** Merged changes provide operation-wide inference-submission reservations, estimated-token admission, provider-serialized output limits, a terminal measured-usage gate, persisted unknown provider outcomes after process interruption, process-crash evidence for selected command/provider paths, and bounded plugin callback execution from PR #932. PR #922 adds per-tool deadline defaults/caps for Rust RPC calls. Local provider inference requests also have a finite whole-request client deadline, with focused Node 24 tests passing 46/46 as recorded above; remote provider processing/billing after local abort remains unknown. These controls do not demonstrate exact token usage or provider spend: input-token admission is heuristic and remote providers may ignore a serialized output cap. Plugin process containment does not impose aggregate CPU/memory quotas or establish host-crash cleanup; crash tests do not establish host/power-loss safety or unknown-outcome recovery for every side-effectful tool/provider path. **WP-P10-02 remains incomplete** for exact/provider-enforced cumulative token containment and recovery qualification for other side-effect classes and host/power failure.\n\n## WP-P10-03 — Acceptance evidence integrity and export\n\n**Priority:** P1 · **Complexity:** M · **Dependencies:** none for local evidence integrity; independent policy identity remains unavailable under WP-P10-01.\n\n**Existing capability:** `FlightRecorder` hash-chains persisted events and `FlightPlayer` reports altered, reordered, removed or unchained records (`cli/src/core/hash_chain.ts`, `cli/src/flight.ts`). GV-2 already exports bounded audit bundles with per-file SHA-256, a canonical manifest, hash-linked file entries, offline verification, and optional operator-key Ed25519 signatures (`cli/src/core/audit_bundle.ts`, `cli/src/audit_contract.ts`). `CandidateTracker` invalidates receipts on known mutations and recomputes the candidate identity before merge (`cli/src/core/candidate.ts`). These controls must be reused; this package does not need a second chain or evidence store.\n\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a22143-fcf9-7462-927e-e66d27ee2285","create_time":1795854504.5379715}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":139980,"sampled_through_sequence":139977,"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
