{"timestamp":"2026-11-28T08:47:52.878Z","ordinal":318225,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","turn_id":"01a22143-fcf9-7462-927e-e66d27ee2285","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-8cb9a538-99ae-4cb0-84dc-429a73448c94","process_id":"12996","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","rg -n -A5 -B2 '^\\*\\*WP-P10-02 status and remaining limits|^## WP-P10-03' docs/assurance/POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-wt/p10-02-file-crash-evidence","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"search","cmd":"rg -n -A5 -B2 '^'\"\\\\*\\\\*WP-P10-02 status and remaining limits|\"'^## WP-P10-03' docs/assurance/POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md","query":"^\\*\\*WP-P10-02 status and remaining limits|^## WP-P10-03","path":"POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"91-**Plugin execution boundary (closed by PR #932):** The pre-fix findings above showed that asynchronous TypeScript plugin hooks could outlive their caller and that late middleware continuation could dispatch a tool after timeout. PR #932 (`3394b3a429d806256ab07936d62ed0586eaec907`, merged as `d0276535118aa197fc56371ab697220e20d5fd43`) moves file-loaded plugin callbacks into persistent, killable worker processes using versioned JSON IPC, a 1 MiB message cap, a 64 KiB combined stdout/stderr cap, and finite per-callback deadlines (default 15 seconds, maximum 60 seconds). Middleware continuation remains parent-owned; malformed or late messages fail closed; possibly-started custom tools and continuations report unknown outcomes; cancellation and shutdown retire workers. Workspace plugins run from a private temporary copy of the exact approved bytes. Local `make build`, `npm run build`, and `npm run typecheck` passed; the 82-test plugin/trust/policy compatibility set passed on Node 24.11.1, and focused containment tests passed 13/13 on both Node 22.22.2 and Node 24.11.1. Adversarial coverage includes synchronous infinite loops, never-settling callbacks, late continuation, timeout after continuation, cancellation, malformed/oversized IPC, and ordinary/detached child cleanup. Required PR checks passed at the exact head (Node 22/24 gates and matrices, all eight test shards, Linux packaging, and CodeQL); post-merge CI run `46107372969` and CodeQL run `46107372886` passed on merge commit `d0276535118aa197fc56371ab697220e20d5fd43`. This is execution containment, not an OS security sandbox or CPU/memory quota. Windows/macOS process-tree cleanup, host-crash cleanup, aggregate resource limits, and same-user deliberate protocol manipulation remain unproven.\n92-\n93:**WP-P10-02 status and remaining limits:** Merged changes provide operation-wide inference-submission reservations, estimated-token admission, provider-serialized output limits, a terminal measured-usage gate, persisted unknown provider outcomes after process interruption, process-crash evidence for selected command/provider paths, and bounded plugin callback execution from PR #932. PR #922 adds per-tool deadline defaults/caps for Rust RPC calls. Local provider inference requests also have a finite whole-request client deadline, with focused Node 24 tests passing 46/46 as recorded above; remote provider processing/billing after local abort remains unknown. These controls do not demonstrate exact token usage or provider spend: input-token admission is heuristic and remote providers may ignore a serialized output cap. Plugin process containment does not impose aggregate CPU/memory quotas or establish host-crash cleanup; crash tests do not establish host/power-loss safety or unknown-outcome recovery for every side-effectful tool/provider path. **WP-P10-02 remains incomplete** for exact/provider-enforced cumulative token containment and recovery qualification for other side-effect classes and host/power failure.\n94-\n95:## WP-P10-03 — Acceptance evidence integrity and export\n96-\n97-**Priority:** P1 · **Complexity:** M · **Dependencies:** none for local evidence integrity; independent policy identity remains unavailable under WP-P10-01.\n98-\n99-**Existing capability:** `FlightRecorder` hash-chains persisted events and `FlightPlayer` reports altered, reordered, removed or unchained records (`cli/src/core/hash_chain.ts`, `cli/src/flight.ts`). GV-2 already exports bounded audit bundles with per-file SHA-256, a canonical manifest, hash-linked file entries, offline verification, and optional operator-key Ed25519 signatures (`cli/src/core/audit_bundle.ts`, `cli/src/audit_contract.ts`). `CandidateTracker` invalidates receipts on known mutations and recomputes the candidate identity before merge (`cli/src/core/candidate.ts`). These controls must be reused; this package does not need a second chain or evidence store.\n100-\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":868826185}},"started_at_ms":1795855672008,"completed_at_ms":1795855672878}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-28T08:47:53.561Z","ordinal":318226,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a22156-e8d8-7e50-ac9e-42e2174546a0","call_id":"call_3217ee26dd5c476980381bf65fea0cff","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.8 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"91-**Plugin execution boundary (closed by PR #932):** The pre-fix findings above showed that asynchronous TypeScript plugin hooks could outlive their caller and that late middleware continuation could dispatch a tool after timeout. PR #932 (`3394b3a429d806256ab07936d62ed0586eaec907`, merged as `d0276535118aa197fc56371ab697220e20d5fd43`) moves file-loaded plugin callbacks into persistent, killable worker processes using versioned JSON IPC, a 1 MiB message cap, a 64 KiB combined stdout/stderr cap, and finite per-callback deadlines (default 15 seconds, maximum 60 seconds). Middleware continuation remains parent-owned; malformed or late messages fail closed; possibly-started custom tools and continuations report unknown outcomes; cancellation and shutdown retire workers. Workspace plugins run from a private temporary copy of the exact approved bytes. Local `make build`, `npm run build`, and `npm run typecheck` passed; the 82-test plugin/trust/policy compatibility set passed on Node 24.11.1, and focused containment tests passed 13/13 on both Node 22.22.2 and Node 24.11.1. Adversarial coverage includes synchronous infinite loops, never-settling callbacks, late continuation, timeout after continuation, cancellation, malformed/oversized IPC, and ordinary/detached child cleanup. Required PR checks passed at the exact head (Node 22/24 gates and matrices, all eight test shards, Linux packaging, and CodeQL); post-merge CI run `46107372969` and CodeQL run `46107372886` passed on merge commit `d0276535118aa197fc56371ab697220e20d5fd43`. This is execution containment, not an OS security sandbox or CPU/memory quota. Windows/macOS process-tree cleanup, host-crash cleanup, aggregate resource limits, and same-user deliberate protocol manipulation remain unproven.\n92-\n93:**WP-P10-02 status and remaining limits:** Merged changes provide operation-wide inference-submission reservations, estimated-token admission, provider-serialized output limits, a terminal measured-usage gate, persisted unknown provider outcomes after process interruption, process-crash evidence for selected command/provider paths, and bounded plugin callback execution from PR #932. PR #922 adds per-tool deadline defaults/caps for Rust RPC calls. Local provider inference requests also have a finite whole-request client deadline, with focused Node 24 tests passing 46/46 as recorded above; remote provider processing/billing after local abort remains unknown. These controls do not demonstrate exact token usage or provider spend: input-token admission is heuristic and remote providers may ignore a serialized output cap. Plugin process containment does not impose aggregate CPU/memory quotas or establish host-crash cleanup; crash tests do not establish host/power-loss safety or unknown-outcome recovery for every side-effectful tool/provider path. **WP-P10-02 remains incomplete** for exact/provider-enforced cumulative token containment and recovery qualification for other side-effect classes and host/power failure.\n94-\n95:## WP-P10-03 — Acceptance evidence integrity and export\n96-\n97-**Priority:** P1 · **Complexity:** M · **Dependencies:** none for local evidence integrity; independent policy identity remains unavailable under WP-P10-01.\n98-\n99-**Existing capability:** `FlightRecorder` hash-chains persisted events and `FlightPlayer` reports altered, reordered, removed or unchained records (`cli/src/core/hash_chain.ts`, `cli/src/flight.ts`). GV-2 already exports bounded audit bundles with per-file SHA-256, a canonical manifest, hash-linked file entries, offline verification, and optional operator-key Ed25519 signatures (`cli/src/core/audit_bundle.ts`, `cli/src/audit_contract.ts`). `CandidateTracker` invalidates receipts on known mutations and recomputes the candidate identity before merge (`cli/src/core/candidate.ts`). These controls must be reused; this package does not need a second chain or evidence store.\n100-\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a22143-fcf9-7462-927e-e66d27ee2285","create_time":1795855673.560768}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":140146,"sampled_through_sequence":140143,"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
