us-wt/p10-02-edit-file-crash-recovery","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"body_file=$(mktemp); cat > \"$body_file\" <<'EOF'\n## Summary\n\n- Generalize the existing process-crash fixture so it can exercise both `write_file` and `edit_file`.\n- Add an adversarial case where the Rust `edit_file` mutation completes, then the process dies before the operation-end journal record is persisted.\n- Verify recovery marks the outcome unknown and preserves the completed file contents without automatically dispatching the tool again.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Focused `p10_process_crash_recovery.test.ts` — 4/4 passed on Node 24.11.1.\n- `npm run typecheck` — passed.\n- `git diff --check` — passed.\n- The Rust helper binary was built in an isolated target directory for the immediately preceding test tranche; PR #937 changes only the crash test file, not the Rust helper source.\n\n## Reference coding\n\nXERJ searches covered Nexus operation token admission and peer provider token budgets. They returned no applicable peer crash-recovery pattern (the peer results were low relevance), so this change reuses Nexus's existing durable crash fixture and Rust `edit_file` handler. No external code was copied. The existing fixture is the closest implementation analogue.\n\n## Limits\n\nThis adds one Rust filesystem mutation path to process-crash coverage. It does not establish host or power-loss durability, every side-effect class, or exactly-once behavior when a model explicitly chooses to repeat an operation whose outcome is unknown.\nEOF\ngh pr create --base release/v1.3.1 --head codex/p10-02-edit-file-crash-recovery --title \"test: cover edit-file crash recovery\" --body-file \"$body_file\""}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/939\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":2,"nanos":757164913}},"started_at_ms":1795856182238,"completed_at_ms":1795856184995}}
