{"timestamp":"2026-11-28T09:44:07.930Z","ordinal":319674,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","turn_id":"01a22143-fcf9-7462-927e-e66d27ee2285","session_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","root_turn_id":"01a22143-fcf9-7462-927e-e66d27ee2285","response_id":"resp_033f353a673dbf89016b0aa260cdb887d28a518364749872eb","usage":{"input_tokens":108362,"cached_input_tokens":107264,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":358,"reasoning_output_tokens":269,"total_tokens":108720},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":38597874,"cached_input_tokens":38115712,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":54036,"reasoning_output_tokens":22507,"total_tokens":38651910},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":6644669038,"cached_input_tokens":6569941504,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":15299883,"reasoning_output_tokens":9001060,"total_tokens":6659968921}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-28T09:44:11.798Z","ordinal":319675,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","turn_id":"01a22143-fcf9-7462-927e-e66d27ee2285","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-d219afb0-d480-4e24-872a-617e3433a1b0","process_id":"66586","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '91,99p' docs/assurance/POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md && git status --short --branch"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-wt/p10-02-edit-file-evidence","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"sed -n '91,99p' docs/assurance/POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md && git status --short --branch"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"**Plugin execution boundary (closed by PR #932):** The pre-fix findings above showed that asynchronous TypeScript plugin hooks could outlive their caller and that late middleware continuation could dispatch a tool after timeout. PR #932 (`3394b3a429d806256ab07936d62ed0586eaec907`, merged as `d0276535118aa197fc56371ab697220e20d5fd43`) moves file-loaded plugin callbacks into persistent, killable worker processes using versioned JSON IPC, a 1 MiB message cap, a 64 KiB combined stdout/stderr cap, and finite per-callback deadlines (default 15 seconds, maximum 60 seconds). Middleware continuation remains parent-owned; malformed or late messages fail closed; possibly-started custom tools and continuations report unknown outcomes; cancellation and shutdown retire workers. Workspace plugins run from a private temporary copy of the exact approved bytes. Local `make build`, `npm run build`, and `npm run typecheck` passed; the 82-test plugin/trust/policy compatibility set passed on Node 24.11.1, and focused containment tests passed 13/13 on both Node 22.22.2 and Node 24.11.1. Adversarial coverage includes synchronous infinite loops, never-settling callbacks, late continuation, timeout after continuation, cancellation, malformed/oversized IPC, and ordinary/detached child cleanup. Required PR checks passed at the exact head (Node 22/24 gates and matrices, all eight test shards, Linux packaging, and CodeQL); post-merge CI run `46107372969` and CodeQL run `46107372886` passed on merge commit `d0276535118aa197fc56371ab697220e20d5fd43`. This is execution containment, not an OS security sandbox or CPU/memory quota. Windows/macOS process-tree cleanup, host-crash cleanup, aggregate resource limits, and same-user deliberate protocol manipulation remain unproven.\n\n**WP-P10-02 status and remaining limits:** Merged changes provide operation-wide inference-submission reservations, estimated-token admission, provider-serialized output limits, a terminal measured-usage gate, persisted unknown provider outcomes after process interruption, process-crash evidence for selected command/provider paths, and bounded plugin callback execution from PR #932. PR #922 adds per-tool deadline defaults/caps for Rust RPC calls. Local provider inference requests also have a finite whole-request client deadline, with focused Node 24 tests passing 46/46 as recorded above; remote provider processing/billing after local abort remains unknown. These controls do not demonstrate exact token usage or provider spend: input-token admission is heuristic and remote providers may ignore a serialized output cap. Plugin process containment does not impose aggregate CPU/memory quotas or establish host-crash cleanup; crash tests do not establish host/power-loss safety or unknown-outcome recovery for every side-effectful tool/provider path. **WP-P10-02 remains incomplete** for exact/provider-enforced cumulative token containment and recovery qualification for other side-effect classes and host/power failure.\n\n**Filesystem side-effect crash tranche (merged PR #937):** `cli/test/p10_process_crash_recovery.test.ts` now kills a durable agent after `write_file` has returned but before its operation-end record can be persisted. Recovery identifies the operation as interrupted, presents its outcome as unknown, and does not replay the completed write. The focused file passed 3/3 on Node 24.11.1; TypeScript typecheck passed. The exact PR head `00dc078fdc16268247baec0a02801b318b63d099` passed required Node 22/24 gates and all eight shards, Linux, JavaScript/TypeScript, and CodeQL after a retry. The first attempt had one unrelated failure in `upgrade_transaction_recovery.test.ts:813`; that test passed in a focused local rerun and the hosted shard retry passed. PR #937 merged as `419bca1a3f899d9b9d1a30b46c2093ceb2383492`; post-merge CI run `46126488514` and CodeQL run `46126488620` passed on that exact merge commit. Entire Gates failed as an advisory check and did not block the merge under repository policy. This demonstrates one filesystem write/process-crash boundary only; it does not demonstrate host/power-loss behavior or unknown-outcome recovery for every side-effect class. **WP-P10-02 remains incomplete.**\n\n## WP-P10-03 — Acceptance evidence integrity and export\n\n**Priority:** P1 · **Complexity:** M · **Dependencies:** none for local evidence integrity; independent policy identity remains unavailable under WP-P10-01.\n## codex/p10-02-edit-file-evidence...origin/release/v1.3.1\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":3,"nanos":758173414}},"started_at_ms":1795859048039,"completed_at_ms":1795859051798}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-28T09:44:12.600Z","ordinal":319676,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a2218a-7838-75f1-82dd-23714516a13b","call_id":"call_9ad4debbee4a4c48bdaa923c939e5589","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 4.8 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"**Plugin execution boundary (closed by PR #932):** The pre-fix findings above showed that asynchronous TypeScript plugin hooks could outlive their caller and that late middleware continuation could dispatch a tool after timeout. PR #932 (`3394b3a429d806256ab07936d62ed0586eaec907`, merged as `d0276535118aa197fc56371ab697220e20d5fd43`) moves file-loaded plugin callbacks into persistent, killable worker processes using versioned JSON IPC, a 1 MiB message cap, a 64 KiB combined stdout/stderr cap, and finite per-callback deadlines (default 15 seconds, maximum 60 seconds). Middleware continuation remains parent-owned; malformed or late messages fail closed; possibly-started custom tools and continuations report unknown outcomes; cancellation and shutdown retire workers. Workspace plugins run from a private temporary copy of the exact approved bytes. Local `make build`, `npm run build`, and `npm run typecheck` passed; the 82-test plugin/trust/policy compatibility set passed on Node 24.11.1, and focused containment tests passed 13/13 on both Node 22.22.2 and Node 24.11.1. Adversarial coverage includes synchronous infinite loops, never-settling callbacks, late continuation, timeout after continuation, cancellation, malformed/oversized IPC, and ordinary/detached child cleanup. Required PR checks passed at the exact head (Node 22/24 gates and matrices, all eight test shards, Linux packaging, and CodeQL); post-merge CI run `46107372969` and CodeQL run `46107372886` passed on merge commit `d0276535118aa197fc56371ab697220e20d5fd43`. This is execution containment, not an OS security sandbox or CPU/memory quota. Windows/macOS process-tree cleanup, host-crash cleanup, aggregate resource limits, and same-user deliberate protocol manipulation remain unproven.\n\n**WP-P10-02 status and remaining limits:** Merged changes provide operation-wide inference-submission reservations, estimated-token admission, provider-serialized output limits, a terminal measured-usage gate, persisted unknown provider outcomes after process interruption, process-crash evidence for selected command/provider paths, and bounded plugin callback execution from PR #932. PR #922 adds per-tool deadline defaults/caps for Rust RPC calls. Local provider inference requests also have a finite whole-request client deadline, with focused Node 24 tests passing 46/46 as recorded above; remote provider processing/billing after local abort remains unknown. These controls do not demonstrate exact token usage or provider spend: input-token admission is heuristic and remote providers may ignore a serialized output cap. Plugin process containment does not impose aggregate CPU/memory quotas or establish host-crash cleanup; crash tests do not establish host/power-loss safety or unknown-outcome recovery for every side-effectful tool/provider path. **WP-P10-02 remains incomplete** for exact/provider-enforced cumulative token containment and recovery qualification for other side-effect classes and host/power failure.\n\n**Filesystem side-effect crash tranche (merged PR #937):** `cli/test/p10_process_crash_recovery.test.ts` now kills a durable agent after `write_file` has returned but before its operation-end record can be persisted. Recovery identifies the operation as interrupted, presents its outcome as unknown, and does not replay the completed write. The focused file passed 3/3 on Node 24.11.1; TypeScript typecheck passed. The exact PR head `00dc078fdc16268247baec0a02801b318b63d099` passed required Node 22/24 gates and all eight shards, Linux, JavaScript/TypeScript, and CodeQL after a retry. The first attempt had one unrelated failure in `upgrade_transaction_recovery.test.ts:813`; that test passed in a focused local rerun and the hosted shard retry passed. PR #937 merged as `419bca1a3f899d9b9d1a30b46c2093ceb2383492`; post-merge CI run `46126488514` and CodeQL run `46126488620` passed on that exact merge commit. Entire Gates failed as an advisory check and did not block the merge under repository policy. This demonstrates one filesystem write/process-crash boundary only; it does not demonstrate host/power-loss behavior or unknown-outcome recovery for every side-effect class. **WP-P10-02 remains incomplete.**\n\n## WP-P10-03 — Acceptance evidence integrity and export\n\n**Priority:** P1 · **Complexity:** M · **Dependencies:** none for local evidence integrity; independent policy identity remains unavailable under WP-P10-01.\n## codex/p10-02-edit-file-evidence...origin/release/v1.3.1\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a22143-fcf9-7462-927e-e66d27ee2285","create_time":1795859052.6000078}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":140777,"sampled_through_sequence":140774,"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
