{"timestamp":"2026-11-28T10:17:19.615Z","ordinal":320372,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","turn_id":"01a22143-fcf9-7462-927e-e66d27ee2285","session_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","root_turn_id":"01a22143-fcf9-7462-927e-e66d27ee2285","response_id":"resp_033f353a673dbf89016b0aaa2590ac87d288e2b3006a97bf88","usage":{"input_tokens":146814,"cached_input_tokens":145664,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":484,"reasoning_output_tokens":357,"total_tokens":147298},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":52939972,"cached_input_tokens":52400128,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":68285,"reasoning_output_tokens":26777,"total_tokens":53008257},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":6659011136,"cached_input_tokens":6584225920,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":15314132,"reasoning_output_tokens":9005330,"total_tokens":6674325268}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-28T10:17:19.783Z","ordinal":320373,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","turn_id":"01a22143-fcf9-7462-927e-e66d27ee2285","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-015a6dda-65af-4185-ba41-081188459520","process_id":"65472","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","git show origin/release/v1.3.1:docs/assurance/POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md | sed -n '81,160p' | rg -n '^## WP|^\\*\\*Priority|^\\*\\*Gap/evidence|remains incomplete|remains partially open|Remaining scope|Remaining work|Required tests'"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-code","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"git show origin/release/v1.3.1:docs/assurance/POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md | sed -n '81,160p' | rg -n '^## WP|^\\*\\*Priority|^\\*\\*Gap/evidence|remains incomplete|remains partially open|Remaining scope|Remaining work|Required tests'"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"13:**WP-P10-02 status and remaining limits:** Merged changes provide operation-wide inference-submission reservations, estimated-token admission, provider-serialized output limits, a terminal measured-usage gate, persisted unknown provider outcomes after process interruption, process-crash evidence for selected command/provider paths, and bounded plugin callback execution from PR #932. PR #922 adds per-tool deadline defaults/caps for Rust RPC calls. Local provider inference requests also have a finite whole-request client deadline, with focused Node 24 tests passing 46/46 as recorded above; remote provider processing/billing after local abort remains unknown. These controls do not demonstrate exact token usage or provider spend: input-token admission is heuristic and remote providers may ignore a serialized output cap. Plugin process containment does not impose aggregate CPU/memory quotas or establish host-crash cleanup; crash tests do not establish host/power-loss safety or unknown-outcome recovery for every side-effectful tool/provider path. **WP-P10-02 remains incomplete** for exact/provider-enforced cumulative token containment and recovery qualification for other side-effect classes and host/power failure.\n15:**Filesystem side-effect crash tranche (merged PR #937):** `cli/test/p10_process_crash_recovery.test.ts` now kills a durable agent after `write_file` has returned but before its operation-end record can be persisted. Recovery identifies the operation as interrupted, presents its outcome as unknown, and does not replay the completed write. The focused file passed 3/3 on Node 24.11.1; TypeScript typecheck passed. The exact PR head `00dc078fdc16268247baec0a02801b318b63d099` passed required Node 22/24 gates and all eight shards, Linux, JavaScript/TypeScript, and CodeQL after a retry. The first attempt had one unrelated failure in `upgrade_transaction_recovery.test.ts:813`; that test passed in a focused local rerun and the hosted shard retry passed. PR #937 merged as `419bca1a3f899d9b9d1a30b46c2093ceb2383492`; post-merge CI run `46126488514` and CodeQL run `46126488620` passed on that exact merge commit. Entire Gates failed as an advisory check and did not block the merge under repository policy. This demonstrates one filesystem write/process-crash boundary only; it does not demonstrate host/power-loss behavior or unknown-outcome recovery for every side-effect class. **WP-P10-02 remains incomplete.**\n17:**Filesystem side-effect crash follow-up (merged PR #939):** The same real-process recovery harness now covers the Rust `edit_file` path as well as `write_file`. It kills the durable agent after the file mutation returns but before the operation-end journal record is saved, then confirms recovery records an interrupted/unknown outcome and does not replay the mutation. The focused suite passed 4/4 on Node 24.11.1; `npm run typecheck` and `git diff --check` passed. Required PR checks and CodeQL passed on refreshed merge commit `a2b01991881538ee9775be7c76e8a94b39b096f6` (base `e44edcedd3aa711a0d05c058351c8f86946507ba`, PR head `a76b414c120e3342ff638a0b4f8cf8cd707fad55`); all eight Node 22/24 shards, both matrices, Linux, and JavaScript/TypeScript passed. Entire Gates failed as an advisory check. PR #939 merged as `b764b753926759650f971ba41823b9e631305db2`; post-merge CI run `46130593983` and CodeQL run `46130593979` passed on that exact commit. This expands process-crash evidence to two filesystem mutation tools; it still does not demonstrate host/power-loss behavior, exactly-once execution, or unknown-outcome recovery for other side-effect classes. **WP-P10-02 remains incomplete.**\n19:## WP-P10-03 — Acceptance evidence integrity and export\n21:**Priority:** P1 · **Complexity:** M · **Dependencies:** none for local evidence integrity; independent policy identity remains unavailable under WP-P10-01.\n57:**Required tests / acceptance:** Existing tests cover altered, deleted, reordered and truncated events and candidate mutation invalidation. Failure injection confirms an omitted event cannot be followed by an apparently intact chain, sanitizer failure does not preserve that claim, and mandatory receipt-persistence failure prevents verified acceptance. The release/v1.3.1 acceptance-identity slice adds source revision, candidate digest, verifier definition digest, command/result, runtime, and policy identity to durable receipts and verifies bundle retention. Legacy receipt export migration behavior is now tested: older receipts remain readable with their historical status, are not assigned synthesized acceptance identity, and are explicitly identified as missing structured identity. Exact-head CI and post-merge CI/CodeQL are complete for the legacy export follow-up. Same-user bundle mutability and the lack of independent approval remain explicit limitations; local evidence does not provide external immutability or a second-person review.\n61:## WP-P10-04 — Resource admission and cleanup envelope\n63:**Priority:** P1 · **Complexity:** M/L · **Dependencies:** WP-P10-02 for shared task budgets; existing operation-local scheduling is already implemented.\n65:**Gap/evidence:** The earlier claim that `Promise.all` makes operation scheduling unbounded was inaccurate. `ConvergenceScheduler.assign` bounds in-flight units by `effectiveConcurrency`, resource slots, and operation-local CPU/memory reservations (`cli/src/core/scheduler.ts:267-307`); parallel execution defaults to one and requires explicit per-unit requests and a per-operation budget (`cli/src/core/operation_schema.ts:101-119`). `OperationRunner.executeBatch` uses `Promise.all` only for the already-admitted due set (`cli/src/core/operation_runner.ts:276-299`). PR #830 added same-host cross-operation slots for declared inference resources; PR #831 added same-home LSP process slots; the active-client slice below bounds ordinary Nexus client processes per OS user. Remaining gaps are aggregate CPU/memory consumption, session counts hosted inside one client process, write-time disk limits outside session-history archives, provider request/token/cost totals across separate processes, and cleanup/recovery accounting outside the existing lease boundaries. Those current gaps are not evidence that per-operation scheduling is unbounded, and no OS-level host CPU/memory ceiling is claimed.\n73:**Required tests / acceptance:** Existing operation-level tests continue to show that units beyond `maxConcurrentUnits` or explicit CPU/memory budget remain unlaunched. New multi-operation/session tests demonstrate that aggregate configured limits are not exceeded under concurrent admission and that rejected admission has no side effect. Cancellation, process exit, failed persistence, and restart release or reconcile leases without orphan children. Repeated compaction/resume respects a measured active-context bound; retained disk has deterministic quota/retention behavior. Cross-session tests demonstrate no cross-workspace data access. Each claim must identify whether the limit is enforced in-process, across processes, or only per operation.\n77:**Progress (2026-11-27, merged PR #889):** A shared, cross-process active-operation cap now reuses `SharedResourceAdmission.acquireNamed`; it does not add a parallel admission framework. `NEXUS_MAX_SHARED_OPERATION_UNITS` defaults to 4 and accepts operator values from 1 through 64. The aggregate lease is acquired before the provider lease, released if provider admission misses, and held through executor completion and result persistence. Focused tests passed 33/33 and the TypeScript build passed on Node 24.11.1. Required PR checks passed at head `f552c2c885b7fe9d00b9acd1677435f084d20be6` and merge candidate `f07cf89d0e44885dbb24beef6721ac7540212ea5`. Post-merge CI run `45932311657` and CodeQL run `45932311572` passed on merge `0390df6b5ff619201600360ff016364ca0bc6af6`; Entire remained advisory. **WP-P10-04 remains partially open:** this control limits active operation units across cooperating current-version processes using the same admission store. It does not bound aggregate CPU/memory, session count, disk use, provider spend, or processes that do not participate in this lease protocol; older running Nexus versions do not hold the new lease until they exit. Cancellation and crash reconciliation outside the existing lease boundary still need separate evidence.\n79:**Progress (2026-11-27, merged PR #919):** Session history archive writes enforce an aggregate cap equal to the existing 2 GiB retained-state ceiling. `SessionManager.assertSessionArchiveCapacity` scans archive files under the existing state-store guard, rejects unreadable/non-regular entries and scans above 100,000 entries, and refuses a write before archive append or fork creation if it would cross the cap. It preserves existing archives and does not add another cleanup or admission framework. The new `session_archive_bounds.test.ts` covers aggregate accounting across sessions, byte preservation on refusal, fork refusal before state creation, inability to raise the ceiling through options, and empty append behavior at a full quota. With the worktree-local Rust core built, `session_archive_bounds.test.ts`, `session_fork.test.ts`, and `compaction_continuity.test.ts` passed **43/43** on Node 24.11.1 (exit 0); process-death recovery cases and the existing archive-write failure case were included. After the final empty-append guard, the current archive and fork suites passed **10/10**, `npm --prefix cli run build` passed, and `git diff --check` passed. PR #919 merged at `2026-11-27T20:00:55Z` as `a944a4eac448fdd1b08f1adfdca5be5696376ad2`. Required PR-head CI run `46059219684` and CodeQL run `46059219707` passed at exact PR head `d1778581428312574f24057e5b2741e6d3aae802`. Post-merge CI run `46060674463` passed both Node test gates, all eight shards, Linux packaging, and diff coverage; CodeQL run `46060674335` also passed on the exact merge commit. Entire Gates failed on the PR head but is advisory and excluded from required merge gates by project policy. Scope is limited to `*.history.jsonl` archives written by participating current-version Nexus processes; this is not a general disk quota and does not cap other state, external writers, or older running versions. **WP-P10-04 remains partially open** for the broader CPU/memory, session-count, provider-accounting, and cleanup/recovery gaps above.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":36609}},"started_at_ms":1795861039782,"completed_at_ms":1795861039783}}
